    {
      "key": "10af0c47-a463-4529-a959-83ff4d0a794d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10af0c47-a463-4529-a959-83ff4d0a794d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da615333-e22c-482f-87ac-8f039a95330c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da615333-e22c-482f-87ac-8f039a95330c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6ec7271-0144-4909-bedc-e9e975a310b2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the widget, you worked directly with bits to make colors. Someone looking at a photo on a phone gets those colors without ever seeing a 0 or a 1. Something sits between the bits and the color.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6ec7271-0144-4909-bedc-e9e975a310b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3b8c1b2-0d5f-48a7-a733-9904e8a8edac",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *When you made colors in the widget, what did you have to work with?*\n- *When someone views a photo on a phone, what do they never have to think about?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that they worked directly with bits in the widget, while a person viewing an image does not have to. Layers of abstraction make that possible by hiding details that are not needed for a given purpose. Move on once students name that the bits are hidden from the viewer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3b8c1b2-0d5f-48a7-a733-9904e8a8edac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e0a1826-86e9-46e3-8a6c-8d364b2e563f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation. As each layer appears, ask students what that layer lets someone ignore. Then direct students to record the layers of abstraction in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e0a1826-86e9-46e3-8a6c-8d364b2e563f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08c28601-a77b-4ada-be37-0d41bc7acf04",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A digital image is made up of several layers that work together to turn bits into color. This is abstraction. Each layer hides details that the layer above it does not need, which is why you can look at a photo without thinking about bits. Let's watch a video of how this works.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08c28601-a77b-4ada-be37-0d41bc7acf04",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76ac5972-2424-4095-a172-8ee63d02ae37",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the How Computers Work Video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76ac5972-2424-4095-a172-8ee63d02ae37",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5928e1fb-b06b-4812-88f7-42ba07f38070",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you have to teach a computer to spot fish in the ocean, but the only way you can tell it anything is by writing rules. Make your rules specific enough that a computer could follow them with no guessing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5928e1fb-b06b-4812-88f7-42ba07f38070",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a298b121-4892-4e35-ba59-d042e36b81a9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students write three rules on scratch paper. Give them 2 minutes to write silently, then 1 minute to compare with a neighbor."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a298b121-4892-4e35-ba59-d042e36b81a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aedf8035-835b-4583-88d1-705c3c14dcbf",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the pictures and give students 2 minutes to check each one against their three rules.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which pictures broke your rules?*\n- *Did any rule accept something that is not a fish, or reject a real fish?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that rules written by people miss cases nobody planned for, and that fixing one rule can break another. Many students' rules will overlap (lives in water, has fins, swims), which shows how a few shared assumptions can all fail on the same unexpected picture.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say the rules just need to be more detailed, ask, \"What rule would you add for the dolphin? Would it break for any real fish?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aedf8035-835b-4583-88d1-705c3c14dcbf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a98ee845-fc70-44ef-a02d-a946bb1229af",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a98ee845-fc70-44ef-a02d-a946bb1229af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe5b0a72-476f-42bb-bfe0-06991583b545",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe5b0a72-476f-42bb-bfe0-06991583b545",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bab563d0-2ade-4b41-93e1-9ca43a651be5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students train an A.I. with their own labels so they experience how a model learns from examples, then give them the vocabulary to describe it: AI system, model, and the difference between rules-based and learned behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Instruct students to take out their copy of the CSP Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Train the AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bab563d0-2ade-4b41-93e1-9ca43a651be5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93754f08-7aa2-4d6a-96b8-ca8ecdecafe0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"What is Machine Learning\".",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Alternatives to AI For Oceans:** AI for Oceans was originally developed as an Hour of Code activity that can be completed by students with any device available. We have modified it for its usage here. Depending on your classroom situation, you might opt to replace the activity with:\n\n**Teachable Machine** - *Teachable Machine is a web-based tool that makes creating machine learning models fast, easy, and accessible to everyone. Teachable Machine is flexible – use files or capture examples live. It’s respectful of the way you work. You can even choose to use it entirely on-device, without any webcam or microphone data leaving your computer.*\n\nIf your classrooms devices have cameras, Teachable Machines offers an engaging way to create training sets. Encourage students to teach the machine to represent rock, paper, or scissors with hand gestures. What are some possible ways for bias to enter in?\n\n**Machine Learning for Kids** - *This free tool introduces machine learning by providing hands-on experiences for training machine learning systems and building things with them. It provides an easy-to-use guided environment for training machine learning models to recognise text, numbers, images, or sounds.*\n\nMachine Learning for Kids is a great option if your students want to work with text samples. Teach the machine to recognize words or passages that are happy or sad. Lots to play around with here!"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93754f08-7aa2-4d6a-96b8-ca8ecdecafe0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9c23f04-b6a2-4918-8043-99d80dedbd6a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Machine learning refers to a computer that can recognize patterns and make decisions. In this activity you’re going to supply the data to train your own machine learning model. Imagine an ocean that contains creatures like fish, but also contains trash dumped by humans. What if we could train a computer to tell the difference and then use that technology to help clean the ocean?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9c23f04-b6a2-4918-8043-99d80dedbd6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5091401-bdef-4018-8949-d0ffd49fd294",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-3. Students should spend around five minutes total on these levels.\n- **Level 1:** Students train the AI to recognize \"fish\" or \"not fish\"\n- **Level 2:** Students see what happens when their training from level 1 is used to determine if other sea creatures belong in the ocean.\n- **Level 3:** Students train the AI on the broader question of what belongs in the water.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Train the AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt their thinking with the question \"What potential problems do you think come up when creating training sets?\""
          },
          {
            "type": "contentCorner",
            "markdown": "Every image in this part of the tutorial is fed into a neural network that has been pre-trained on a huge set of data called ImageNet. The database contains over 14 million hand-annotated images. ImageNet contains more than 20,000 categories with a typical category, such as \"balloon\" or \"strawberry\", consisting of several hundred images. When A.I. is scanning new images and making its own predictions in the tutorial, it is actually comparing the possible categories for the new image with the patterns it found in the training dataset."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5091401-bdef-4018-8949-d0ffd49fd294",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12a7b9c5-080a-4d6c-926d-1fcf47bfc491",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *How well did A.I. do?*\n- *How do you think it decided what to include in the ocean?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the A.I. only knew what their labels taught it. Answers about how well it did will vary, so the aim is to connect the results to the examples they supplied."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12a7b9c5-080a-4d6c-926d-1fcf47bfc491",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14b9fd6b-fa59-4a10-a93a-fb36902d0107",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"Training Data & Bias\"."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14b9fd6b-fa59-4a10-a93a-fb36902d0107",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a54b3f3f-dbf6-4ecc-ac89-4efc38f60367",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How do you think your training data influenced the results that A.I. produced?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the A.I. could only learn from the examples they gave it. When the training data left out sea creatures, the A.I. called them \"not fish,\" so students' choices as trainers shaped what it got right and wrong."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a54b3f3f-dbf6-4ecc-ac89-4efc38f60367",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc52740d-f39d-4c46-8ba0-42b2a63e0f10",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The fish-finder you just trained is an AI system. It took in pictures and produced a decision: fish or not fish.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc52740d-f39d-4c46-8ba0-42b2a63e0f10",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ffa65b1-3fae-41fc-8fcd-2f5b2be17b67",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *AI System*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ffa65b1-3fae-41fc-8fcd-2f5b2be17b67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "633d3e03-03a0-488b-bc81-e2b6a4df33e6",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Inside the fish-finder, the model is the part that looks at a picture and decides. Your warm-up rules were one kind of model. The one you trained with labels is another kind.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "633d3e03-03a0-488b-bc81-e2b6a4df33e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c41c7e99-ff94-47c7-aff2-7a4c02390ffa",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *model*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c41c7e99-ff94-47c7-aff2-7a4c02390ffa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c58b3a86-5981-472b-a9ed-2ece10063a7f",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember when the trained A.I. called sea creatures \"not fish\"? It had no examples of them. That is the learned version of a rule nobody wrote.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c58b3a86-5981-472b-a9ed-2ece10063a7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82ba69c1-a4b1-4ca4-a028-9b4c39b9574c",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the table using students' own warm-up rules and the Levels 1-3 results.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Learned models still depend on human choices, because people pick the examples and the labels. That sets up the bias discussion that comes next."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82ba69c1-a4b1-4ca4-a028-9b4c39b9574c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f05eee2e-0266-479d-ba3b-7a792f8c761f",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students train an A.I. on a word that depends on opinion so they see their own judgments become the training data. Help them connect that experience to real computing innovations and to what designers and developers can do to reduce bias and consider the effects of their programs.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Bias in Training Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f05eee2e-0266-479d-ba3b-7a792f8c761f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77ee3044-6927-469d-9cf1-ba00e66cfb34",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you will teach A.I. about a word of your choosing by showing it examples of that type of fish. As before, A.I. doesn't start with any training data about these labels. Even though the words in this level are fairly objective, it's possible that you will end up with different results based on their training data. You might even intentionally train A.I. incorrectly to see what happens!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77ee3044-6927-469d-9cf1-ba00e66cfb34",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2dae29af-dc95-4f7b-8fd5-c2f4ebd38a23",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 4-5. Students should spend around five minutes total on these levels.\n- **Level 4:** Students train the AI to recognize a new word of their choice.\n- **Level 5:** Students train the AI on a new, subjective word of their choice.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Bias In Training Data",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt their thinking with the question \"Is it fair to use AI to judge something by looks? What unintended bias could this cause?\" Here, as before, students will use training data to teach A.I. to recognize different types of fish. The words in this list are intentionally more subjective than what students will have seen so far. Encourage students to decide for themselves what makes a fish look \"angry\" or \"fun\". Two students may choose the same label and get a very different set of results based on which fish traits were their focus. Encourage students to discuss their findings with each other or go back and choose new words. Each student will rely on their own opinions to train A.I. which means that A.I. will learn with the same biases held by the students. As students begin to see the role their opinion is playing, ask them to reflect on whether this is good or bad, and how it might be addressed."
          },
          {
            "type": "contentCorner",
            "markdown": "The fish in this tutorial are randomly generated based on some pre-defined components, including mouths, tails, eyes, scales, and fins, with a randomly chosen body color, shape, and size. Rather than looking at the actual image data, A.I. is now looking for patterns in these components based on how the student classifies each fish. It will be more likely to label a fish the same way the student would have if it has matching traits."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2dae29af-dc95-4f7b-8fd5-c2f4ebd38a23",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a4ff22cb-b448-488e-8c4d-d8e3cae51685",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"How I’m Fighting Bias in Algorithms\" with Joy Buolamwini.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This clip stops before Joy's recommendations. If students ask what can be done about algorithmic bias, share her three ideas: who codes matters (diverse teams who can check each other's blind spots), how we code matters (building fairness into the system), and why we code matters (making social change a priority)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a4ff22cb-b448-488e-8c4d-d8e3cae51685",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffa031a3-b212-476b-a2e6-564e5b46488f",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *How can computing innovations which make use of Machine Learning reflect existing human bias?*\n- *How could it be used to discriminate against groups of individuals?*\n- *How can that bias be minimized?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that machine learning can reflect human bias because people choose the training data and write the algorithms, and that collecting more data does not remove bias. They should begin to suggest ways to reduce it, such as more diverse training examples."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffa031a3-b212-476b-a2e6-564e5b46488f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c0c4c33-bfb9-44d7-bf12-f0b62b1b1410",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 6 to answer the checking for understanding question.",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to check whether students can explain how training data can lead to unfair results, recognize that the app learns from examples instead of following rules, and describe a step designers can take to reduce bias before launch."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c0c4c33-bfb9-44d7-bf12-f0b62b1b1410",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b44f67c-f546-4dd3-9bcc-2d39a5d72ec4",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b44f67c-f546-4dd3-9bcc-2d39a5d72ec4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7b788fa-9df6-4348-816e-ed479fa5e2d6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7b788fa-9df6-4348-816e-ed479fa5e2d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ffb352ba-d9e3-4c1f-85ca-e3aca06621c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34f1e773-dfbc-436e-a43d-9e7dbbf15268",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34f1e773-dfbc-436e-a43d-9e7dbbf15268",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ffb352ba-d9e3-4c1f-85ca-e3aca06621c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce2b85d7-679d-4005-a3c9-677ecfc2eba3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *A spam filter deletes any email that contains the word \"free.\" What is the model in this AI system, and is it rules-based or learned? How do you know?*\n- *How would the filter change if it learned from emails that people marked as spam?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the model is the part of the filter that decides which emails to delete, and that the first filter is rules-based because people wrote the rule. Students should recognize that the second filter is learned because it finds patterns in examples, and that each kind fails in its own way: the rule misses messages nobody wrote a rule for, while the learned filter depends on which examples it was given.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say the learned filter is simply better, ask, \"What if the emails people marked as spam were incomplete or unfair?\" This connects back to training data and bias from the activity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce2b85d7-679d-4005-a3c9-677ecfc2eba3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ffb352ba-d9e3-4c1f-85ca-e3aca06621c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15e28f78-356c-4a8b-9646-f5e6e273974e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15e28f78-356c-4a8b-9646-f5e6e273974e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ffb352ba-d9e3-4c1f-85ca-e3aca06621c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "569da64d-71a1-41c6-98ea-fcebd1e119b2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover next word prediction by doing it themselves, before generative AI or LLMs are named.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a set of sentence starter cards.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students in pairs or small groups. One student draws a sentence starter card and reads it aloud (e.g. \"The weather today is...\"). Without discussing, everyone else silently writes the word they think comes next."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "569da64d-71a1-41c6-98ea-fcebd1e119b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c6094a87-79fb-4e2d-9fb3-f192dada6fea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a131cf2-d54d-4510-bb81-af5bcee14912",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What word did each person write?*\n- *Where did you agree? Where did you disagree*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice agreement on predictable starters and disagreement on ambiguous ones. Some words are simply more likely to come next than others.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students describe their guesses as \"obvious\" or \"random,\" that is the vocabulary you want. Reuse \"likely\" and \"pattern\" when you introduce generative AI next."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a131cf2-d54d-4510-bb81-af5bcee14912",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c6094a87-79fb-4e2d-9fb3-f192dada6fea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36888e4c-5ed9-4142-8edc-f7495950bef7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36888e4c-5ed9-4142-8edc-f7495950bef7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c6094a87-79fb-4e2d-9fb3-f192dada6fea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48d6ee54-84bf-4986-a0c2-1aa63838f13b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48d6ee54-84bf-4986-a0c2-1aa63838f13b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c6094a87-79fb-4e2d-9fb3-f192dada6fea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a791822b-9aa3-49fd-bea0-133b44e683be",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Practice applying the concept of disclosure to real examples, and notice that not every case has an obvious answer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *generative AI*.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Disclosure Voting"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a791822b-9aa3-49fd-bea0-133b44e683be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34be3888-402d-4166-8a22-a4ce97832609",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that LLMs generate text by predicting likely word sequences, the same thing students just did in the warm up."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34be3888-402d-4166-8a22-a4ce97832609",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4a583f1-c652-410e-a0c5-4356e3fcd15e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce that publishing content involves more than creating it. Creators must consider whether AI contributed, whether attribution is required, what license governs the content, and whether permission is needed before sharing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4a583f1-c652-410e-a0c5-4356e3fcd15e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5cce912-c340-4b21-b454-e844db7b9f32",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** For each example, give students about 30 seconds to vote. Have them defend their reasoning with a partner before discussing as a class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the pace brisk, about 30 seconds per example. Save deeper debate for the whole class discussion at the end."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5cce912-c340-4b21-b454-e844db7b9f32",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45c3f4f3-572b-44ee-bd55-4c50636d714b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the example. Students vote and defend with a partner."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45c3f4f3-572b-44ee-bd55-4c50636d714b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "059e0fc6-7df7-4225-af2b-f857915099c4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the example. Students vote and defend with a partner."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "059e0fc6-7df7-4225-af2b-f857915099c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "619c32fb-36df-4766-9c02-80d2b52c9113",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the example. Students vote and defend with a partner.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This example tends to split the room. Let a few different votes speak before moving on."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "619c32fb-36df-4766-9c02-80d2b52c9113",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "caad952a-d3c2-4ab5-9ed2-d46f89ccc51e",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the example. Students vote and defend with a partner."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "caad952a-d3c2-4ab5-9ed2-d46f89ccc51e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bea986fc-736f-4bf7-849d-7c6f5be19941",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the example. Students vote and defend with a partner."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bea986fc-736f-4bf7-849d-7c6f5be19941",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31a9657a-8ab4-4198-ba90-b0b4ed776aa3",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the example. Students vote and defend with a partner.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This one is often confusing since the AI enhanced real content instead of creating something new. Let that tension surface in the discussion."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31a9657a-8ab4-4198-ba90-b0b4ed776aa3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73abba32-3574-4ff8-b89c-cd19d82bebf9",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Which examples were easy calls? Which were hard?*\n- *What made the difference?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that stakes, audience, and intent affect whether disclosure feels necessary. Connect back to the attribution and licensing ideas from the direct instruction."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73abba32-3574-4ff8-b89c-cd19d82bebf9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b30422f4-bd0f-45d3-abfd-e2ff2570c993",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Investigate how different AI disclosure labels affect trust, and what information a badge does and doesn't communicate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of 2-3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the Media Investigation Guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "What’s Behind the AI Badge?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b30422f4-bd0f-45d3-abfd-e2ff2570c993",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5c50c8a-8a04-4be8-9de6-ee574cfb7cd7",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** In small groups, students examine social media posts with different disclosure labels. Using the Media Investigation Guide, groups answer what the badge tells them, what it doesn't tell them, whether it increases or decreases trust, whether they'd verify before sharing, and whether their decision would change with no label."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5c50c8a-8a04-4be8-9de6-ee574cfb7cd7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f210fb2b-fa10-4181-bc13-2cc3d49dbe66",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did you notice about how different labels affected your trust?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Different disclosure systems communicate different amounts of information. A badge alone doesn't guarantee accuracy or trustworthiness."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f210fb2b-fa10-4181-bc13-2cc3d49dbe66",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a617e969-b783-492e-84cb-eef1e607032d",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that realistic AI media, including deepfakes, can mislead audiences if shared without context."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a617e969-b783-492e-84cb-eef1e607032d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a81032fb-c2d0-49f3-90e7-afd73a8dd0f8",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that responsible publishing includes verifying information, disclosing AI use when appropriate, respecting licensing requirements, and considering potential harm before sharing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a81032fb-c2d0-49f3-90e7-afd73a8dd0f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f601cf0f-fe4a-4a3d-b183-1ea48360ce15",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Step into the role of a content reviewer and apply the same decision-making process across multiple types of media before deciding whether each is ready to publish.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a Publication Decision Guide and a media folder.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Ready to Publish?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f601cf0f-fe4a-4a3d-b183-1ea48360ce15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8637291-b6d7-4189-90ef-9fe059ee3ab5",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** In small groups, students use the Publication Decision Guide to evaluate every item in the folder before deciding whether it is ready to publish. Groups justify each decision and explain which safeguards they applied."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8637291-b6d7-4189-90ef-9fe059ee3ab5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9e100cd-7bed-4435-939c-75a72d752873",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which item was hardest to decide on? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Different media types raise different safeguards. A photo needs less scrutiny than a deepfake, and a Creative Commons work needs attribution even though it wasn't made with AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9e100cd-7bed-4435-939c-75a72d752873",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d9880ed0-76cd-4c78-93ac-cdad7f8d378a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d9880ed0-76cd-4c78-93ac-cdad7f8d378a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00c69a9b-15d6-4bb3-ac4e-76ce28851aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac0bad98-2dd2-40b9-8880-249c94df191d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac0bad98-2dd2-40b9-8880-249c94df191d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00c69a9b-15d6-4bb3-ac4e-76ce28851aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6b15c50-21e6-43ea-830c-a6fae31c702b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6b15c50-21e6-43ea-830c-a6fae31c702b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00c69a9b-15d6-4bb3-ac4e-76ce28851aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1f81caf-7765-466c-8791-1b3e6c579713",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover, through voting on real messages, that a confidence score is not the same as being correct — and that catching what the bot misses takes a human who actually reads for tone and context. This is exactly how AI moderation fails in the real world: missing slang it was never trained on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario. Explain that students are acting as admins for a large gaming Discord community whose AI moderation bot has flagged four messages, each with a toxicity score and a suggested action. Clarify that the toxicity score is how confident the bot is, not a fact about the message — a high score just means the bot is sure, not that it's right."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1f81caf-7765-466c-8791-1b3e6c579713",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e94b3792-d9cd-4f61-bb3e-0048f8195c5e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three-part checklist. Tell students that before deciding on any flagged message, they run it through Tone, Context, and Stakes.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Tone might be sarcasm read as an insult, Context might be a long-time member versus a brand-new account, and Stakes might be a joke that could still get someone temporarily banned."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e94b3792-d9cd-4f61-bb3e-0048f8195c5e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7073f795-0088-4933-a6fa-052f7b5ecba5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** For each message, students vote with a hand signal: thumbs up to approve the bot's action, thumbs down to override it, or sideways to escalate to a human moderator."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7073f795-0088-4933-a6fa-052f7b5ecba5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a555d755-782e-4cc8-92a9-001f95c1ab8e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the first message aloud, then have students vote by hand signal: approve, override, or escalate. Call on a couple students to share a reason before revealing the next message."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a555d755-782e-4cc8-92a9-001f95c1ab8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "345e2fbf-afae-4447-b70d-a04705d78ef8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the second message aloud, then have students vote by hand signal: approve, override, or escalate. Call on a couple students to share a reason before revealing the next message."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "345e2fbf-afae-4447-b70d-a04705d78ef8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f5060bc-7c42-4c4e-a600-4925b782bdec",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the third message aloud, then have students vote by hand signal: approve, override, or escalate. Call on a couple students to share a reason before revealing the next message.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Watch for students overriding this one out of habit from the last two messages. If most of the class overrides it, pause here rather than waiting for the pattern slide, and ask what is actually different about this sender."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f5060bc-7c42-4c4e-a600-4925b782bdec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5395fb9c-c301-4259-9647-3f9188b65a2d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the final message aloud, then have students vote by hand signal: approve, override, or escalate. Call on a couple students to share a reason before moving to the discussion."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5395fb9c-c301-4259-9647-3f9188b65a2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfa23007-55a8-405a-8df2-f3406f34a0b7",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What do you notice about the messages you overrode? Is there a pattern?*\n- *What does the bot actually need?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that every override involved a veteran member using slang the bot was never trained on, while the one real problem came from a brand-new account — no need for the term \"model drift\" yet. On the fix, guide them to see that retraining alone or adjusting the model alone won't work; the bot needs new data that includes this community's slang, then needs to be retrained on it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfa23007-55a8-405a-8df2-f3406f34a0b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12829413-cccc-47fe-9ff1-5c7ddbcf1e64",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12829413-cccc-47fe-9ff1-5c7ddbcf1e64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe65ae0a-b176-4d7d-9ec3-c842666cfc97",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe65ae0a-b176-4d7d-9ec3-c842666cfc97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c0c4e16-5c0c-4441-8c42-4078ca13e779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b71f8bbe-d9f5-478d-8a1a-159b0280744c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students name what they just experienced in the warm-up — model drift — then build the vocabulary and toolkit (the four ways things can go wrong, the four possible actions) they'll need to diagnose and respond to events during today's simulation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to take out their Unit 1 Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Build Your Toolkit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b71f8bbe-d9f5-478d-8a1a-159b0280744c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2968d58f-a49a-47fa-9e81-6493e7da7cab",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define model drift. Point back to the Auto-Mod bot from the warm-up — its accuracy on veteran members using new slang is drift in action. Have students record the definition in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2968d58f-a49a-47fa-9e81-6493e7da7cab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15f1c166-8ffb-44a7-830e-a9696a9b1f22",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define each of the four categories. Tell students these are the four things they'll need to diagnose during today's activity.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Don't preview which action fixes each category — figuring that out themselves is the point of Keep It Running. This slide should name the four problems, not the four solutions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15f1c166-8ffb-44a7-830e-a9696a9b1f22",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "031b99ae-882d-4479-812b-33594b418fc2",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that a model can't catch its own drift. A human monitoring performance is what catches it, and a human decides how to respond.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point back to two moments students already experienced: in the warm-up, the bot never noticed its own pattern of mistakes — a human (them) had to catch it. In \"Catching Drift Takes a Human,\" it's the same idea, just named as a standard: humans are the ones who monitor, and humans are the ones who decide what happens next."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "031b99ae-882d-4479-812b-33594b418fc2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a4056aa4-e49b-4600-9dac-a85cb1cbb96d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *transparency statement*. Have students record the definition in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a4056aa4-e49b-4600-9dac-a85cb1cbb96d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3b10a66-13c6-498e-8713-ef24317c50a1",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the slide to tell students that an AI system at launch is not the same system a year later. Tell them they're about to spend a year finding out exactly what that means.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is the moment to make explicit that students are about to be the human choice this slide describes — every action they take in the simulation is one of the differences between their system at launch and their system a year later."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3b10a66-13c6-498e-8713-ef24317c50a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61f4feb5-98a6-4590-9a40-c2938b5b583d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the four actions students will choose between during today's simulation. Do not tell students which action fixes which type of problem — figuring that out is the point of the activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61f4feb5-98a6-4590-9a40-c2938b5b583d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "396b1e7d-0122-4420-bd4f-4b464144f33f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students diagnose what's happening to their AI system and choose how to respond, then connect that experience back to the warm-up and to real AI systems they actually use during the debrief.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair students up before starting. Partners work through Level 1 together, discussing each event and deciding how to respond as a team.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 14, Levels 1 to complete Level 1 with their partner. Students should complete Level 2 individually.\n- **Level 1:** Students go through four rounds of the simulation. Each round is one of the four ways things can go wrong.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a check for understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for justifications that don't actually explain the reasoning (a single word, or a restatement of the action) — ask pairs to say their reasoning out loud before typing it. Also watch for pairs clicking \"Skip\" on a stale transparency statement field without noticing why it's flagged.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Keep It Running",
        "progression_name": "Keep It Running",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "An optional Activity Guide is available for Level 1. It isn't required — experiencing the simulation is meant to be a learning experience, not a formative assessment. If you'd like students to turn in evidence of their work, you can distribute the activity guide to students which gives them two options: screenshot the Year in Review summary screen, or copy and paste the summary text."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2 checks all three lesson objectives. The Decision That Mattered checks that students can name a real human decision from their own gameplay and its effect — the core of the standard. Spot the Drift checks whether they can recognize model drift in a new scenario and explain how humans should respond. What Users Deserve to Know checks whether they can describe what a transparency statement must include and why it matters to users."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "396b1e7d-0122-4420-bd4f-4b464144f33f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51a9d105-9bf2-444c-80d8-8616389bd2bb",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Where did you see the same kind of mistake happen in both the warm-up and the simulation — a system getting something wrong because the world around it changed, not because it was broken?*\n- *Where have you seen this happen in a real AI system you've actually used?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to name the shared thread explicitly: in the warm-up, the bot misread messages because gaming slang had shifted beyond what it was trained on; in the simulation, at least one event (drift or usage shift) put their system in the same position — right at launch, wrong once the world moved. The system in both cases wasn't \"broken,\" it just hadn't caught up. For the second question, listen for real examples like a recommendation algorithm missing a new trend, a spam filter missing new scam wording, or a voice assistant struggling with an accent or slang it wasn't trained on — the specific example matters less than whether students can correctly identify it as the same pattern: real-world change outpacing what a system was built to handle."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51a9d105-9bf2-444c-80d8-8616389bd2bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e418905a-5d47-46c1-bccc-7fdfa990949e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "288eb784-a170-4cf2-b26a-429c50c2f2f5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "288eb784-a170-4cf2-b26a-429c50c2f2f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f6de41ec-03e2-4fe8-882c-99335f62b73f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22462a03-0c3e-444c-9a24-e7037796eb69",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Give students thirty seconds to write one word on scrap paper, a sticky note, or the back of their Unit Guide that answers the Question of the Day. On your count of three, everyone holds their word up at once.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Scan the room as words go up. Call out three or four that capture the lesson well, and briefly address any that reveal a misunderstanding before moving on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22462a03-0c3e-444c-9a24-e7037796eb69",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f6de41ec-03e2-4fe8-882c-99335f62b73f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88071db8-a4b8-4cf9-a54b-20e54ccc78dc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88071db8-a4b8-4cf9-a54b-20e54ccc78dc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f6de41ec-03e2-4fe8-882c-99335f62b73f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b11c03e4-440c-4593-9edc-0b296ab7ca43",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we are going to try our own hands at cropping and then closely examine a real-world situation where training a model to crop photos leads to algorithmic bias.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b11c03e4-440c-4593-9edc-0b296ab7ca43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a29dc74-fcf2-49f2-a52c-db1912cdbcef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "410818f6-3b4f-4567-988d-55b88db51465",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- Think about a time when you shared a picture with your friends or on social media.\n- *Have you ever had to crop a photo you shared?*\n- *How did you decide what to crop out of the picture?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Use this discussion to show that users usually have a reason for sharing photos and the focus depends on that reason. Students will most likely bring up a wide range of examples that can be used to highlight how there isn’t a one size fits all approach to sharing photos and cropping them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "410818f6-3b4f-4567-988d-55b88db51465",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a29dc74-fcf2-49f2-a52c-db1912cdbcef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ae6dc01-4804-401a-9d23-abc9dc895b3f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ae6dc01-4804-401a-9d23-abc9dc895b3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a29dc74-fcf2-49f2-a52c-db1912cdbcef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a8e22c7-f3a9-4af3-a5b4-00ef5e27e0cf",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a8e22c7-f3a9-4af3-a5b4-00ef5e27e0cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a29dc74-fcf2-49f2-a52c-db1912cdbcef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f0e6f9c-9568-47d0-9d30-859154dccd8b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students crop images as if they were posting to social media, then compare choices with a partner to see that different people make different decisions about what to keep and what to leave out.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 15, Level 1 to crop the images as if they were uploading them to a social media account. Students can also upload their own images and crop them as well.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Cropping Widget",
        "progression_name": "Cropping Widget"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f0e6f9c-9568-47d0-9d30-859154dccd8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "765e0bae-60da-4810-92ea-f45f846ce4b5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which images were challenging for you to decide how to crop?*\n- *Which images did you and your partner crop the same?*\n- *Which images did you and your partner crop differently?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Use this discussion to highlight the different experiences and perspectives everyone brings to deciding how to crop an image. Direct students to consider not only what they decided to keep in the picture, but also what they decided to crop out of the picture. What happens when we crop something out of a picture? It never gets seen by the audience. Look back at the photos you cropped. What or who got left out? You may choose to guide the class toward describing general characteristics of images that were challenging to crop."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "765e0bae-60da-4810-92ea-f45f846ce4b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d55f2e1c-47ab-4e56-adcf-a11774390e24",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now imagine we recorded how everyone in this room cropped the images from this widget. We could use that information to train a model, just like we did with the fish earlier in the unit. This model could then be used to crop other photos being uploaded to the social media site.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d55f2e1c-47ab-4e56-adcf-a11774390e24",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60ced984-c474-4088-83e6-3ebd09f81a2e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How might the cropping data from our classroom be biased?*\n- *What are some ways we could address the biases in our data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that if this were training a model, bias could arise from a few different places.\n- The limited set of pictures included in the app that are most likely not representative of the photos being shared on social media.\n- The choices of the students themselves. Are teenagers more likely to focus on certain things when cropping? Is this representative of the whole population of social media users?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When discussing bias, connect back to the training students did earlier in the unit when deciding what an ‘angry’ or ‘fun’ fish looks like, pushing students to consider how their opinions might lead to problems when training an AI model that would be widely used."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60ced984-c474-4088-83e6-3ebd09f81a2e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96463f00-6336-41b8-ae30-756f03707372",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students watch a real case of a cropping algorithm that favored some people over others, then identify the evidence and the sources of bias.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Twitter Cropping Algorithm Video"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96463f00-6336-41b8-ae30-756f03707372",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3e71e09-4ca9-45a5-ac7a-ef436beb1ad3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It turns out that the issue of how to crop an image is something social media platforms have been working on for some time. We are going to watch a video that shows the issues that arose when Twitter used machine learning to train an algorithm to do this cropping.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3e71e09-4ca9-45a5-ac7a-ef436beb1ad3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3e46ac5-badd-4737-b25f-100f3724b157",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Are We Automating Racism?\" video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3e46ac5-badd-4737-b25f-100f3724b157",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c5927d3-6357-4518-af2f-192c113377c3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the second part of the video “Are We Automating Racism?”  (from 9:30 - 11:01)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c5927d3-6357-4518-af2f-192c113377c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f7d5b68-48dd-462e-acd6-cc833680462e",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Next, the reporter talks with an engineer who built a similar cropping tool, and then asks what data it was trained on.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f7d5b68-48dd-462e-acd6-cc833680462e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c01cb2ff-0d7a-4103-8292-588984b36249",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the the third part of the video “Are We Automating Racism?”  (from 12:52 - 13:46)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c01cb2ff-0d7a-4103-8292-588984b36249",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57e72533-45f6-47fd-9144-5f6beba66404",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *bias*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57e72533-45f6-47fd-9144-5f6beba66404",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f5018b5a-702c-4230-8b14-a3b72dbffaf0",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data* *bias*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f5018b5a-702c-4230-8b14-a3b72dbffaf0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74eaad59-3856-4724-a3c0-0d3fbba84e91",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Who did the cropping algorithm favor, and what evidence in the video shows this?*\n- *Using our definition, is this bias? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the reporters' tests and the tweets show the algorithm consistently kept lighter faces over darker faces, which fits the definition of bias. Students should recognize that the harm is about who gets seen, not just whether the crop was accurate.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep students focused on the evidence and on what the algorithm did, rather than on whether the machine itself is racist."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74eaad59-3856-4724-a3c0-0d3fbba84e91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60e83b06-8386-4089-9e27-d47e6b524f02",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *How was the Twitter cropping algorithm trained?*\n- *According to the video, where is a potential source of bias when training similar cropping algorithms?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the machine is not prejudiced. The bias came from design choices and data. Students should recognize that the model learned from saliency datasets built from photo libraries that do not represent the population as a whole (data bias), and that people chose that approach. Push students to explain how the two interact: choices about what data to collect shape how the app behaves for different users."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60e83b06-8386-4089-9e27-d47e6b524f02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61d309d1-6107-4d54-80d1-7052812df684",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students read and annotate an article to find where bias was identified, what Twitter tested, and what Twitter changed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Twitter Cropping Algorithm article",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Twitter Cropping Algorithm Article"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61d309d1-6107-4d54-80d1-7052812df684",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "614364d1-1ed0-4aac-9fd2-ef658869fb0b",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've seen how the problem was discovered, we are going to read how it played out and how Twitter responded when presented with evidence of bias in their cropping algorithm.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "614364d1-1ed0-4aac-9fd2-ef658869fb0b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8cad8c1e-8796-4336-b796-1e88afad0708",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students read the article \"Twitter says its image-cropping algorithm was biased, so it's ditching it (CNN).\" After they are finished reading, they should mark up the text with the following:\n- Mark: Where Twitter found bias, what Twitter tested, and what Twitter changed\n- At The End: Write a 10-word summary of the article",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To Print or Not To Print? This lesson is written assuming that you have printed out the article and have it physically available for students to write on, even though it is also possible to have students interact with this text digitally. If students read the article digitally, it is most important that they still follow the active reading strategies outlined in this lesson - highlighting the text, writing in the margins, and summarizing. This may require some additional time & instruction to teach students your preferred tools of digital annotation, and may require some additional adjustments to some of the later annotation strategies in this lesson."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8cad8c1e-8796-4336-b796-1e88afad0708",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59440aec-4b67-42ca-8a3d-223cf3cdadb2",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students show what they understand about how bias enters an app and when to automate or augment, then propose a specific revision to a biased design and describe how to retest it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 15, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- **Level 2:** Students identify how a human choice and a data choice can lead to an unfair result.\n- **Level 3:** Students decide how much an algorithm should be trusted with a high-stakes task.\n- **Level 4:** Students propose a revision to the cropping algorithm and describe how to retest it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As you walk around, listen for students tying their Level 4 change to a specific source of bias from the video, and for retests that include the people the algorithm treated unfairly. If students get stuck, ask which part of the algorithm they think caused the problem, and what photos they would run through the new version.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Check For Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check For Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 to check whether students can connect a human choice about what data to collect to an unfair result for some users.\n\nUse Level 3 to check whether students can weigh speed and consistency against the stakes of a mistake, recognize when a task needs human judgment, and keep a person responsible for the final decision.\n\nUse Level 4 to check whether students can propose a specific revision tied to a source of bias, describe test photos that include the people affected, and state what result would show improvement."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59440aec-4b67-42ca-8a3d-223cf3cdadb2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa82c15a-e8d3-437d-a528-12952210f240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d5cc207-e263-4626-8bb4-25cd37dd1485",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d5cc207-e263-4626-8bb4-25cd37dd1485",
        "lesson_activity.key": "47a1a4c1-fdb6-47e3-aa31-6f146bbbaf3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55f4dba6-a116-4fa7-85d1-0b06242ce9d6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55f4dba6-a116-4fa7-85d1-0b06242ce9d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "47a1a4c1-fdb6-47e3-aa31-6f146bbbaf3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cfcd97dc-443d-4ebf-875d-9e168c8a066c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "After testing, Twitter's team found bias in the cropping algorithm and largely stopped using it to crop photos. Here is one of the concerns they raised. Notice that they say the concern remains even if the algorithm treated every group equally. Take a moment to read it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cfcd97dc-443d-4ebf-875d-9e168c8a066c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "47a1a4c1-fdb6-47e3-aa31-6f146bbbaf3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "276b5dd6-fe15-4516-a9f7-aeaaa3eb4957",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read aloud, or instruct students to read, the response from Rumman Chowdhury on behalf of Twitter.\n\nSource: https://blog.twitter.com/engineering/en_us/topics/insights/2021/sharing-learnings-about-our-image-cropping-algorithm",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may not know \"representational harm.\" If they ask, explain that it is harm that comes from how people are shown, or whether they get to show themselves the way they want to be seen."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "276b5dd6-fe15-4516-a9f7-aeaaa3eb4957",
        "lesson_activity.key": "47a1a4c1-fdb6-47e3-aa31-6f146bbbaf3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "913578d9-9f88-4433-8276-ace60d21e978",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The story of Twitter and its photo cropping algorithm shows how something built with one intention, in this case to better highlight photos, can lead to unintended effects, in this case bias that favored lighter faces. It also shows a company taking action by establishing a machine learning ethics, transparency, and accountability team led by Rumman Chowdhury.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "913578d9-9f88-4433-8276-ace60d21e978",
        "lesson_activity.key": "47a1a4c1-fdb6-47e3-aa31-6f146bbbaf3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "761dcea2-f3dd-42c9-8e80-d0058cc78dc1",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- Do you think Twitter's response was appropriate?\n- What are the risks and potential harms of systems, such as social media platforms like Twitter, becoming too dependent on machine learning?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that Twitter weighed the speed and consistency of automated cropping against the risk of representational harm, and concluded that cropping depends on what each person wants to show. Students should recognize that tasks involving personal judgment about how people are represented are poor candidates for automation, even when automation is faster.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say Twitter should simply have fixed the algorithm, point them back to the quote on the previous slide. Ask what concern would still remain even if the algorithm treated every group equally."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "761dcea2-f3dd-42c9-8e80-d0058cc78dc1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "47a1a4c1-fdb6-47e3-aa31-6f146bbbaf3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a95534b-0c78-4b90-bb84-7368361e8eee",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a95534b-0c78-4b90-bb84-7368361e8eee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "47a1a4c1-fdb6-47e3-aa31-6f146bbbaf3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb8d5630-481e-49ef-909b-7e36d499c11d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Hook students into today’s scenario and surface that “accurate” isn’t an obvious, fixed standard — it’s something people have to define, and different people might define it differently. This sets up why Step 1 (defining criteria) matters before any testing happens.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What would count as a “good” answer here — and who decides?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should start to notice that “good” isn’t obvious or universal — it might depend on who’s asking (a student wanting a fast answer vs. a librarian needing it to be exact), and that someone has to decide on a standard before anyone can even test whether the chatbot meets it. There’s no right answer expected here — just a couple of volunteer responses to get students thinking about criteria before they define their own later in the lesson.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this to under 2 minutes — it’s a quick hook, not a full discussion. One or two student responses is enough before moving to the lesson objectives."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb8d5630-481e-49ef-909b-7e36d499c11d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a9775704-295d-4438-99a3-aa3c3cc42e5d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3b05a7d-fefb-4cc1-8bb1-0e213f669b4c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3b05a7d-fefb-4cc1-8bb1-0e213f669b4c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a9775704-295d-4438-99a3-aa3c3cc42e5d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9de879b1-918f-413d-ab0c-18fc2ee418ca",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day. Let students know this question comes back at the end of the lesson, after they test an AI chatbot themselves.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "No need to discuss this now — just pose it and move on. Students don’t have enough information yet to answer it meaningfully; the point is to give them something to hold onto and revisit once they’ve done the testing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9de879b1-918f-413d-ab0c-18fc2ee418ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a9775704-295d-4438-99a3-aa3c3cc42e5d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a4fe70c-ffa4-46ef-9169-f6c984642e6f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students define what “accurate” means for a real AI use case, then write test questions that put their own criteria to the test.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the AI Lab activity guide.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Design Test Criteria"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a4fe70c-ffa4-46ef-9169-f6c984642e6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f3ec4ce-8d16-4f89-abc5-fef7bcb07241"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2c061ce-7894-4259-8ee1-e0c440c961cf",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Step 1 on their activity guide. Have students work with their partner to decide what a correct answer needs to include, what would make an answer unacceptable, and write their team’s accuracy criteria — the standard they use to score the chatbot’s responses later in Step 3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for criteria that are too vague to actually score against later, like “it should be good” or “it should make sense.” Push pairs toward something specific and checkable — does it need to match a fact exactly? Does it need to admit when it’s not sure? If a pair is stuck, ask: “If the chatbot gave a confident-sounding answer that was wrong, how would you even know?”",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If needed, provide an example or two, but don’t over-scaffold this step — it’s meant to be student-generated. A pair that writes weaker criteria now still gets a valid learning experience in Step 4, when they discover their criteria let an inaccurate answer slip through as a “Meets.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2c061ce-7894-4259-8ee1-e0c440c961cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f3ec4ce-8d16-4f89-abc5-fef7bcb07241"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5bf62aa-09d8-4e5e-b028-eda0c3f3d0f6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Step 2 on their activity guide. Have them continue working with their partner and write six required test questions, making sure to cover all four categories listed — not just easy ones — plus up to two optional backup questions they aren’t required to test.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make sure teams are covering all four categories, not just easy ones. Watch for pairs who write six questions that are all variations of the same type (e.g., all easy fact-lookups) — if you spot this, point them back to the four categories and ask which ones they haven’t tried yet."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5bf62aa-09d8-4e5e-b028-eda0c3f3d0f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f3ec4ce-8d16-4f89-abc5-fef7bcb07241"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a70399b-784c-4254-9bdb-5eee6b30b07f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test their own questions against the chatbot, score each response using their own criteria, then compare results with their partner to find a pattern in what breaks the system.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 16, Level 1, to complete Steps 3 and 4 in their activity guide. Partners split their six questions and each test and score their own three on their own page in Step 3. In Step 4, partners bring their pages back together to compare results and identify a pattern.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for pairs scoring responses based on how confident or polished the answer sounds, rather than checking it against the Quick Reference sheet or their own Step 1 criteria. If a team marks every response “Meets,” ask them to point to the specific line on the reference sheet that confirms it. Also watch for teams rushing past Step 4 without actually comparing both partners’ pages side by side.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Test, Score, and Reflect",
        "progression_name": "Test, Score, and Reflect",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Steps 3–4 on the activity guide can be used as a formative check of the lesson objectives (specifically Skill 1.C / LO 1.3.B). Look for scoring that references specific evidence (the reference sheet or their own criteria) rather than general impressions, and a Step 4 pattern that correctly identifies where the chatbot lacked information rather than where it simply sounded off."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a70399b-784c-4254-9bdb-5eee6b30b07f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f3ec4ce-8d16-4f89-abc5-fef7bcb07241"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4bc9cd79-3aeb-49ef-8a10-2314bd1ac3e9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 16, Levels 2-4.\n- **Level 2:** Students reflect on whether the library chatbot is accurate enough to trust without human review, using evidence from their own testing.\n- **Level 3:** Students think about who would be affected if a patron had relied on the chatbot’s holiday-hours mistake.\n- **Level 4:** Students apply that same judgment to a new scenario — a parks department chatbot — and explain their reasoning.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** On Level 2, watch for students giving a blanket yes/no without citing a specific example from their own testing — prompt them to point to a response they actually scored. On Level 3, watch for vague answers like “people could be confused” — push students toward a specific, plausible person or consequence tied to the actual scenario. On Level 4, watch for students who answer without explaining why this situation differs from the ones that worked fine during testing, or who don’t connect their answer back to anything they noticed with the library chatbot.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Check for Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 2–4 serve as a formative check of today’s lesson objectives. Level 2 shows whether students can judge accuracy using their own evidence rather than general impressions. Level 3 shows whether students can name a concrete, plausible consequence rather than a vague one. Level 4 shows whether that judgment transfers to a new situation — look for students who recognize the live, individual data problem (the permit application) as the same kind of gap as the library chatbot’s holiday blind spot, rather than giving a disconnected answer."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4bc9cd79-3aeb-49ef-8a10-2314bd1ac3e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f3ec4ce-8d16-4f89-abc5-fef7bcb07241"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f463433-9ff6-4cf3-ab88-b99cf6200438",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f463433-9ff6-4cf3-ab88-b99cf6200438",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d73a1550-97b9-48f1-94ba-98f6b21cb7c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "499cf3f0-0a17-488a-9707-a3fb51f49937",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to think about what they learned today. Facilitate a whole-class discussion — invite a few students to share the pattern their team found in Step 4, and a few others to share their answer from Level 3 about who would be affected. Tie the discussion back to today’s lesson objectives. Listen for students connecting specific chatbot behavior to real consequences, not just general statements about AI being risky."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "499cf3f0-0a17-488a-9707-a3fb51f49937",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d73a1550-97b9-48f1-94ba-98f6b21cb7c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d310b50c-f51a-44b3-bcf5-896d9102848b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we're going to look at what it takes to write \"good\" instructions. We all use instructions all the time, whether it's directions to get somewhere, instructions to fill out a form, or even instructions from teachers. To help us get started, let's brainstorm what \"bad\" instructions\" look like.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d310b50c-f51a-44b3-bcf5-896d9102848b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b53d2627-f9c6-414e-bf60-ecad249a0135"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ab6bb30-b360-41c8-8b66-a43c3e5e0dbf",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Write down three different reasons you would call a set of instructions \"bad\". Be ready to share with a neighbor.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Aim to hear a few different students share reasons that instructions are \"bad\". The point here is just to get students thinking and there's no specific answer you're driving towards. Some possible ideas, however, might include:\n- Instructions are not clear on what to do\n- Instructions use confusing words\n- Instructions don't actually accomplish what they're supposed to",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the Retrieve-Pair-Share strategy to facilitate this discussion by directing students to write down their thoughts and ideas first, then pair up students to compare their answers and expand their notes. This allows time for individual processing and idea generation before sharing thoughts with others. Ask students to share the ideas they discussed with their partners as a class."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ab6bb30-b360-41c8-8b66-a43c3e5e0dbf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b53d2627-f9c6-414e-bf60-ecad249a0135"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd852a3a-15af-485f-b63d-a8c408fa578d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd852a3a-15af-485f-b63d-a8c408fa578d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b53d2627-f9c6-414e-bf60-ecad249a0135"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "809dd416-e295-48c6-95a7-98ca02fca12e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "809dd416-e295-48c6-95a7-98ca02fca12e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b53d2627-f9c6-414e-bf60-ecad249a0135"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4859f522-1b78-4569-8bb6-3fcac7ed2409",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students write text-only instructions for a block design, then try to rebuild another group's design from their instructions alone, so they experience firsthand how ambiguous everyday language can be.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of two, optionally forming a group of three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a small set of blocks (either LEGO or paper cutouts). Also have each group use a blank sheet of paper or a blank page in a journal.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Lego Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson is students' first introduction to the concept of a programming language. It helps them understand why programming languages exist by giving them a first-hand experience with the problems that programming languages are designed to address. Natural language is usually too ambiguous for giving precise instructions that can be followed correctly 100% of the time. We need to create more structured and precise programming languages in order to accomplish this."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4859f522-1b78-4569-8bb6-3fcac7ed2409",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d402c53f-ec08-492a-aed4-e13b7d9c71b8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a couple minutes for Step 1. They will use their lego pieces to create their own design. Keep this quick since the bigger focus should be on the instructions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d402c53f-ec08-492a-aed4-e13b7d9c71b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d7635cc-aef9-48f7-9eaf-4630913030eb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students move to Step 2. Either have students sketch their design or take a picture. Make sure the image is on a separate piece of paper or the back side of a sheet of paper."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d7635cc-aef9-48f7-9eaf-4630913030eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fb21d1a-d168-4688-a16b-6967ecb11a63",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students move to Step 3. This is the most important step and should be given about 5 minutes. Encourage students to be as clear as possible while only using words. Encourage them to think about their brainstorm of \"bad\" instructions at the start of class to see if it can inspire them to make \"good\" ones."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fb21d1a-d168-4688-a16b-6967ecb11a63",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c3ebbc6-39fe-4684-9cca-9c640ebe2892",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Move students to Step 4. Have students take apart their design, and then trade instructions with another group. Make sure they keep their recording of the design hidden."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c3ebbc6-39fe-4684-9cca-9c640ebe2892",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "643094ec-aec1-4234-a329-7b0cdf0384a3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students move to Step 5. Give students 3 minutes to follow the instructions from another group."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "643094ec-aec1-4234-a329-7b0cdf0384a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73c8fbd0-42b3-4c71-a3e8-274c2085b06b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students 2 minutes for Step 6 where they compare what they built with the picture of the actual target. In their groups they should discuss what they think went wrong or anything they're surprised worked out."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73c8fbd0-42b3-4c71-a3e8-274c2085b06b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86828c53-e6b2-440e-9f38-f35b94f2c3cb",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Depending on how much time you have, encourage students to trade with one or two other groups. Make sure to save time for the wrap up.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Make it a Gallery Walk:** Depending on your room set-up you may be able to have students run this activity in a more open-ended way. Students should leave their unconstructed blocks and instructions at their table with the target image face down. They can then make their way to a few different groups' instructions over the course of 10-15 minutes.\n\n**Mark What's Confusing:** You may optionally have students leave suggestions on instructions when they use them explaining where they were confused or wanted more detail. This is a good way to practice giving and receiving feedback. It also forces groups to think more clearly about what is causing their confusion since they'll have to leave it in writing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86828c53-e6b2-440e-9f38-f35b94f2c3cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "834bec7d-229c-454e-88c3-647fe0807bf3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students show what they understand about natural and programming languages by describing how they differ and explaining why programming languages exist.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Unit 1 Lesson 17, Level 1 to complete the Checking for Understanding question.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use these levels to check whether students can describe how natural and programming languages differ, connect ambiguity to the problems they saw in the activity, and justify why programming languages exist."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "834bec7d-229c-454e-88c3-647fe0807bf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "45fb7853-0964-408a-b305-23f3235c3060"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3959dd47-9aac-4488-bec8-71a338c26666",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3959dd47-9aac-4488-bec8-71a338c26666",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70d007d-cd4f-4438-9980-4620b97f8773"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55e8b229-bdbd-4596-83a8-210d5e9f97f3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55e8b229-bdbd-4596-83a8-210d5e9f97f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70d007d-cd4f-4438-9980-4620b97f8773"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8631b6b-6495-4784-ac6a-f7e33b958cdd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** When you or your classmates made mistakes following instructions today what \"went wrong\"? Try to be as specific as possible.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that mistakes came from specific moments when an instruction could be read in more than one way. Students might say things like \"if we'd just been more clear\" or \"we just need to include more detail.\" This might be true, but eventually you should point out that human language is often ambiguous. In other words, it's not their fault! It's hard to do a good job with bad tools!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8631b6b-6495-4784-ac6a-f7e33b958cdd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70d007d-cd4f-4438-9980-4620b97f8773"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92111cc1-eb52-4a60-9342-085d9fcc7baa",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Even trying to be as clear as possible we struggled to write clear instructions. There are things we could do to improve, but the core challenge here is that everyday human language is bad for giving clear instructions. Words can have two or three meanings! Instructions that seem clear turn out to be vague when we actually go follow them. If we want to give clear instructions, we need to fundamentally change the language we use. We need to create a new kind of language.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92111cc1-eb52-4a60-9342-085d9fcc7baa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70d007d-cd4f-4438-9980-4620b97f8773"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0920c376-75b0-41a2-89fd-41f83588f3e9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Imagine we were going to redesign human language to be really good for giving clear instructions. What types of changes would we need to make?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that a language built for giving instructions would need to remove the ambiguity they ran into during the activity. Specific answers aren't important, but push students to build on their experiences in the main activity. For example, if they saw that words having two meanings was confusing, they should suggest redesigning the language so each word has a single well-understood meaning."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0920c376-75b0-41a2-89fd-41f83588f3e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70d007d-cd4f-4438-9980-4620b97f8773"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e4cae88-c145-4256-80f2-131f67d0805e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the slide with the four problems students will need to solve.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Give students one minute to review the problems, then let them move around the room collecting the information they need. This should take about three minutes."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e4cae88-c145-4256-80f2-131f67d0805e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cc640f15-7bae-4590-a1c5-276e4a8eb24c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4240a584-d106-4cb7-8af1-110c83964172",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As computer scientists, it's important to ask “have I seen this problem before?” or “how is this problem similar to others I've solved?”",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4240a584-d106-4cb7-8af1-110c83964172",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cc640f15-7bae-4590-a1c5-276e4a8eb24c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f30ca102-ec38-4675-9d74-b60403835b6c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which problems did you solve in a similar way?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that Problems 1 and 2 are solved the same way: as soon as you find one person who meets the criteria, you're done. Students should recognize that Problems 3 and 4 are solved the same way too: you have to ask every classmate and keep track of the closest birthday you've heard so far.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students share with a partner first, then discuss with the whole room."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f30ca102-ec38-4675-9d74-b60403835b6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cc640f15-7bae-4590-a1c5-276e4a8eb24c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1cd77abb-90b6-4ce6-a563-c3d2b8d1003c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1cd77abb-90b6-4ce6-a563-c3d2b8d1003c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cc640f15-7bae-4590-a1c5-276e4a8eb24c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bde3d9f9-9dea-400d-8a69-550c2434127c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bde3d9f9-9dea-400d-8a69-550c2434127c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cc640f15-7bae-4590-a1c5-276e4a8eb24c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7d53e6a-4a3f-40e1-b2e1-e5ec4b9fc504",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *problem*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7d53e6a-4a3f-40e1-b2e1-e5ec4b9fc504",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "35fee49f-ffb7-41d3-80f5-61a2acbdb5f2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students trace pairs of flowcharts, write out the steps, and decide whether each pair solves the same problem.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Comparing Algorithms"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "35fee49f-ffb7-41d3-80f5-61a2acbdb5f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "819056eb-c8e3-4258-9630-6cc5c48147b2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We just thought about whether problems are similar. Now we're going to look at whether two sets of instructions are solving the same problem.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "819056eb-c8e3-4258-9630-6cc5c48147b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f31c7f2-d6da-402d-a574-bf3475e011f2",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of partners one activity guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f31c7f2-d6da-402d-a574-bf3475e011f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d3e02bc-10ce-4c3f-b814-6bdee1dc00df",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this activity, a robot follows instructions on a grid. It draws a line, called a trail, on every square it moves through. The robot starts facing north, toward the top of the grid. Left and right always mean the robot's left and right, not yours. Each flowchart is a set of instructions. You start at Start, follow the arrows one box at a time, and stop at End.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d3e02bc-10ce-4c3f-b814-6bdee1dc00df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b780c7f-5970-4fde-8488-ff8cd8b41a62",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students often turn relative to the page instead of the robot. Have them turn their bodies, or rotate their paper, to match the robot's facing before they draw."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b780c7f-5970-4fde-8488-ff8cd8b41a62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "505f3945-3375-452c-911d-74a6793468c1",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's trace Flowchart A together. Turn right, so the robot faces east. Move forward, so it draws one segment east. Let's write that as steps: one, turn right. Two, move forward. Now Flowchart B. Turn left three times: north to west, west to south, south to east. Move forward, one segment east. Same trail. The flowcharts look different, but they solve the same problem.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "505f3945-3375-452c-911d-74a6793468c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61639767-c1f7-42e8-b377-986cc8ada0be",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to reveal the answer: Same."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61639767-c1f7-42e8-b377-986cc8ada0be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "325a0a5e-8c1c-434f-8db9-8cffaeb6940b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *algorithm*. Refer back to the two flowcharts from the previous slide as examples of algorithms. To help students get a better understanding of what an algorithm is, make the connection to everyday tasks such as the steps involved in making a sandwich or tying your shoes as algorithms they use."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "325a0a5e-8c1c-434f-8db9-8cffaeb6940b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "829f7421-114f-4022-b9a8-77b0901d9c4c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *sequencing*. Use the two flowcharts in the activity guide as examples of sequencing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "829f7421-114f-4022-b9a8-77b0901d9c4c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5aa16e4b-fbdc-4b78-a6c6-a0863920db0a",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have partners move to evaluate Paris 2 and 3. Partners should split each pair, one tracing Flowchart A and one tracing Flowchart B, then compare and decide together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students turn from the robot's point of view, write one step per box, and use their trails as evidence before deciding.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity to check whether students can trace a flowchart, write its steps in order, and use the result to justify whether two flowcharts are the same."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5aa16e4b-fbdc-4b78-a6c6-a0863920db0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d688b23-cb0a-49a3-ae42-d60ee7805340",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to reveal the answer: Not the same. Both draw an L, but one bends right and the other bends left.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students who judge by shape may call these the same. Have them draw both trails on the same grid."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d688b23-cb0a-49a3-ae42-d60ee7805340",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d95310d-10a0-42a1-8a76-4504bfdeecbb",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to reveal the answer: Same. Two right turns and two left turns both leave the robot facing south, so both draw one segment south."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d95310d-10a0-42a1-8a76-4504bfdeecbb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "660d8313-b04a-4d1d-9a2f-af40bf10a302",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's look at this one together. Flowchart B has a diamond, which asks a question. If the answer is no, the arrow sends the robot back to move again. If yes, it goes to End. Follow the arrows: the robot moves four times, then stops.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "660d8313-b04a-4d1d-9a2f-af40bf10a302",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3722bb91-e073-4f04-8766-8874982d013a",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to reveal the answer: Same. Both draw a line four squares long.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You don't need to teach how loops work. Students only need to follow the arrows."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3722bb91-e073-4f04-8766-8874982d013a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "feb6119b-b827-4708-a4eb-843735b0a1bb",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What did the “same” pairs have in common that the “not the same” pair did not?*\n- *What would you say makes two sets of instructions “the same”?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the “same” pairs drew the same trail even though the flowcharts looked different, with different numbers of boxes, different turns, or a diamond. Students should recognize that Pair 2 looked almost identical but drew different trails. Students should recognize that two sets of instructions are the same when they accomplish the same task, not when they look alike."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "feb6119b-b827-4708-a4eb-843735b0a1bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "694b4687-6d17-45f0-9a19-0756e19b2395",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Check whether students can explain why two algorithms can look different but solve the same problem, define a problem, an algorithm, and sequencing, and match written steps to a flowchart.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Unit 1 Lesson 18, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- **Level 1:** Students look at a new pair of flowcharts and explain whether they solve the same problem, using the trail as evidence.\n- **Level 2:** Students explain the difference between a problem and an algorithm in their own words, and what sequencing means for an algorithm.\n- **Level 3:** Students choose which flowchart matches a set of written steps.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use these levels to check whether students can explain why two algorithms can look different but solve the same problem, define a problem, an algorithm, and sequencing, and match written steps to a flowchart."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "694b4687-6d17-45f0-9a19-0756e19b2395",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c4bccc0f-a177-4909-8dd6-5d6a9e35e7b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bad3e40d-143d-4a3a-ab78-14f67df68a96",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bad3e40d-143d-4a3a-ab78-14f67df68a96",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d255a991-4437-4496-9982-3c1846770b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b946a1a9-ab93-48ee-8d88-4863e31df80f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b946a1a9-ab93-48ee-8d88-4863e31df80f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d255a991-4437-4496-9982-3c1846770b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1a768d9-d64c-48af-9a7b-753522059d30",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Computer scientists don't just think about code, they think about problems and the algorithms that solve them. Next, you'll start writing instructions that a computer can run.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1a768d9-d64c-48af-9a7b-753522059d30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d255a991-4437-4496-9982-3c1846770b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1957e5a-5cbe-4c80-ad30-794c36404239",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How did today’s activities change the way you think about algorithms and problems?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that algorithms are separate from the problems they solve, and that the same problem can be solved in different ways.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students think on their own, share with a partner, then discuss with the whole room."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1957e5a-5cbe-4c80-ad30-794c36404239",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d255a991-4437-4496-9982-3c1846770b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90500c27-72a3-48f3-b0f7-8bebdb87537b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90500c27-72a3-48f3-b0f7-8bebdb87537b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d255a991-4437-4496-9982-3c1846770b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ac61f3b-24ec-42b4-a70c-dd43768ed77e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students draw on what they discovered in the Need for Programming Language lesson — that natural language is ambiguous while programming languages need precise, unambiguous rules — to set up why exact syntax matters in today's coding activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *List two to three differences between a natural language and a programming language.*\n- *Why do those differences need to exist?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should surface ideas like: programming languages have strict, limited syntax while natural language allows many ways to say the same thing; programming languages can't tolerate ambiguity because the computer will only do exactly what's written; natural language relies on context and shared understanding between people, which code can't assume. If students reference the Need for Programming Language lesson (e.g. the Lego activity) to support their answer, that's a strong sign the connection landed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ac61f3b-24ec-42b4-a70c-dd43768ed77e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b6e9a0b-a503-4fd6-a23b-65c1ba20a68d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85a218cb-2790-424b-85fd-276a15f9b6e2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85a218cb-2790-424b-85fd-276a15f9b6e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b6e9a0b-a503-4fd6-a23b-65c1ba20a68d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b60917d7-7283-418a-847d-cc427cf32035",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b60917d7-7283-418a-847d-cc427cf32035",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b6e9a0b-a503-4fd6-a23b-65c1ba20a68d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a523e84-e370-45f6-bd68-a8906b6454f1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore the Neighborhood and the Painter, then predict and run their first program before anything is explained — the notice/wonder discussion and explainer slides that follow only formalize what students already discovered on their own.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to The Neighborhood. Point out that the grid is 8 spaces wide and 8 spaces tall, with coordinates starting at (0, 0) in the top-left.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Explore the Neighborhood",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Reinforce the idea that this is a 2D coordinate system, which they'll use to understand the Painter's movements."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a523e84-e370-45f6-bd68-a8906b6454f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1a0da47-8f05-450b-849b-d39f390b2b6d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out obstacles like the traffic cone, food truck, and sidewalk. Explain that the **Painter** can't move on or through these obstacles."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1a0da47-8f05-450b-849b-d39f390b2b6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62c01798-3a0e-458f-81cd-4e22fb57d39c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the **Painter** as the character students control with code. Emphasize that the **Painter** already has built-in abilities — move, turn, and paint. Students won't define how these actions work; instead, they'll learn how to tell the Painter what to do and when.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask how the Painter knows how to do these things, let them know these abilities are already built in — students will focus on combining and sequencing them, not building them from scratch.\n\nRefer back to the warm-up if needed: \"Just like natural language and programming language have different rules, giving the Painter instructions means being precise and exact — no ambiguity.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62c01798-3a0e-458f-81cd-4e22fb57d39c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23e71858-ad84-44ba-ae97-75f0fb720269",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the block party scenario. Explain that students will help the Painter mark up the street for the event today, and that they'll need to navigate around things already set up in the Neighborhood — like the food truck.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If any students aren't familiar with the term \"block party,\" a one-sentence explanation (a street closed off for a neighborhood celebration) is usually enough — the image should carry most of the meaning."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23e71858-ad84-44ba-ae97-75f0fb720269",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4101fba2-fc6e-4a2e-a385-85c4193fa1b8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 19, Level 1 to read a short program, predict what the Painter will do, choose their answer, then run the code to check.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Notice whether students are actually reading and tracing the code before choosing an answer, or just picking one quickly to unlock Run. If students seem to be guessing, ask them to walk through the code line by line and describe what each line does before locking in a choice.",
        "progression_name": "Predict and Run",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The CodeAI CSP curriculum uses several level types when introducing programming concepts. We do not recommend having students complete the Predict & Run or the Investigate & Modify levels in isolation. Instead, we encourage teachers to have students discuss and problem solve with a partner, for the class to pause and reflect as they work, and use the levels as opportunities to provide just-in-time feedback. Refer to the Guide to Programming Levels for teaching strategies and suggestions for supporting student learning while they engage with these levels."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4101fba2-fc6e-4a2e-a385-85c4193fa1b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "581c964c-b3fe-4a11-959d-b104eaa8f6f0",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What did you notice about the code in this program?*\n- *What do you wonder about the code in this program?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students toward noticing the structural patterns in the code — that each command is written with words followed by parentheses (e.g., move(), turn_left()), that the commands run in order from top to bottom, and that the line starting with # is a comment, not something the Painter does. Students may also notice that only turn_left() appears and wonder whether a turn_right() command exists — encourage this wonder rather than answering it, since it sets up something they'll need to reason through as they work with the code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "581c964c-b3fe-4a11-959d-b104eaa8f6f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea1d107c-8caa-43a8-bfa4-9f62ac788135",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the default conditions for every new Painter — at the top-left corner of the grid (0, 0), facing East, with 0 units of paint.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Clarify that \"faces East\" means moving right on the grid — this is often the first directional detail students mix up."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea1d107c-8caa-43a8-bfa4-9f62ac788135",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68c31b7f-0d77-4202-9172-0ff3052e4bd6",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain the import line. Emphasize that Python doesn't know what a Painter is unless you import it first."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68c31b7f-0d77-4202-9172-0ff3052e4bd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "180f9029-8555-4bc5-93c8-ac37c1ec199d",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain **comments**. Point out that line 1 starts with **#** — ask students if they remember wondering about that line during the Notice/Wonder discussion. Emphasize that comments are ignored when the code runs; they're only there to help the reader."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "180f9029-8555-4bc5-93c8-ac37c1ec199d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0aba203-f18e-422d-ae65-80e4342011cb",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test and formalize what they noticed and wondered about in Predict & Run — tracing, tinkering with, and commenting the code themselves, then naming the vocabulary (programming statement, program, sequential programming) and new commands (take_paint(), paint()) they'll need for Modify.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 19, Level 2 to trace, tinker, and comment on the code from Predict & Run.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who don't revert their turn_left() change back to three after tinkering — the instructions ask them to change it back when done. Check that predictions and comments (lines 8 and 14) reflect actual reasoning, not guesses, and listen for students connecting what they see in Tinker back to the \"no turn_right()\" wonder from the Notice/Wonder discussion.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Investigate",
        "progression_name": "Investigate"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0aba203-f18e-422d-ae65-80e4342011cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d7a1819-bb8d-4aa6-9c96-22a1149a0319",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to take out their copy of the CSP Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d7a1819-bb8d-4aa6-9c96-22a1149a0319",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40cc77c7-5ef0-4b53-a2ae-5eabb55b4ab7",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *programming statement*. Point to the code shown — each line (move(), turn_left()) is a programming statement. Ask students what other \"statements\" they'd expect to see later in the course. Have students annotate the code snippet in their unit guide, circling or highlighting one line and labeling it as a programming statement."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40cc77c7-5ef0-4b53-a2ae-5eabb55b4ab7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4973861-5a83-46af-880d-7dedd8d6f33b",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *program*. Point to the code shown — the whole set of five lines together is a program made up of individual programming statements. Ask students what happens if the statements run in a different order than they're written (connects to what they saw in Tinker). Have students annotate the code snippet in their unit guide, bracketing or highlighting all five lines and labeling them as a program."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4973861-5a83-46af-880d-7dedd8d6f33b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f56c6ca-fa5c-43e4-b8f5-1d42f2051e67",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *sequential programming*. Point to the code shown — the statements run top to bottom, one at a time, with no branching or user input. Ask students to imagine what would happen if computers could run statements in any order they wanted, rather than sequentially. Have students annotate the code snippet in their unit guide, drawing arrows or numbering the lines in the order they run."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f56c6ca-fa5c-43e4-b8f5-1d42f2051e67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8c53666-8eb5-4ecf-a0ef-d49df48e3bf6",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain **set_paint()** and paint(). Emphasize that if the Painter runs out of paint, **paint()** won't paint — but the Painter will still follow the rest of the code and keep moving. Point out that a message will appear in the console letting them know there's no paint left."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8c53666-8eb5-4ecf-a0ef-d49df48e3bf6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "581d4afe-3a78-47b3-a413-961d7ea7bfff",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice applying and extending sequential code independently, then build debugging skills by tracing existing code to find and fix an error.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 19, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 6.\n- **Level 3:** Students add paint commands to the existing code and extend it to finish the path.\n- **Level 4:** Students choose one of four scenarios and write code to finish painting the street to the cone.\n- **Level 5:** Students find and fix a bug in existing code.\n- **Level 6:** Students answer a check for understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students trying to use a turn_right() command in Levels 3–5 — remind them only turn_left() exists and ask what combination of left turns gets the same result. In Level 4, some students may hesitate to commit to one scenario; reassure them any of the four options is fine and they don't need to try multiple. In Level 5, students who say the code \"doesn't work\" without pinpointing where should be prompted to trace it line by line, comparing each step to where the painter actually ends up. For students who finish early, ask them to try a different Level 4 scenario or explain their Level 5 fix to a partner.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Modify: Extend and Debug",
        "progression_name": "Modify: Extend and Debug",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 5 (Debug) and Level 6 (CFU) can both be used as formative assessments — Level 5 checks whether students can trace code to locate and correct an error, and Level 6 checks whether students can predict a painter's ending position and direction from a short sequence of commands."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "581d4afe-3a78-47b3-a413-961d7ea7bfff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c86fc435-25fd-4bff-86de-75af9d6475d8",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What would happen if you ran your Level 3 or 4 code out of order — would the painter still end up in the same place?*\n- *Why did tracing the code line by line help you find the bug in Level 5?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that because code runs sequentially — one statement at a time, top to bottom — the order of commands directly determines the outcome, which is exactly why tracing line-by-line was an effective way to find the bug in Level 5. This ties directly back to the \"sequential programming\" vocabulary from earlier in the lesson and sets up why order will matter even more once conditionals and loops are introduced in Unit 2."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c86fc435-25fd-4bff-86de-75af9d6475d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3df3b22-fd61-4a89-bc2e-b4e21fb7a108"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a02e3236-9d97-408d-a776-856497e25b87",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a02e3236-9d97-408d-a776-856497e25b87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4dd8fc7e-fd17-4a27-9bed-5da798779f01"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70fbae0a-13f0-450b-8790-d58a7e0f00a4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a moment to silently think about the Question of the Day and what they learned today. Let them know they'll use these reflections in the next activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70fbae0a-13f0-450b-8790-d58a7e0f00a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4dd8fc7e-fd17-4a27-9bed-5da798779f01"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbeb8197-eb99-4834-be75-63bcad429e40",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students turn to a partner and share their Glows, Grows, and Want-to-Knows from today's lesson, using their thinking from the Question of the Day to help shape their answers. Cold-call one or two pairs to share out with the class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Glows, Grows, Want-to-Knows is a strategy to help students identify their strengths and areas of improvement and share any questions or misconceptions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbeb8197-eb99-4834-be75-63bcad429e40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4dd8fc7e-fd17-4a27-9bed-5da798779f01"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4104c2cb-96fe-4ab8-bf06-3466e92966d6",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4104c2cb-96fe-4ab8-bf06-3466e92966d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4dd8fc7e-fd17-4a27-9bed-5da798779f01"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e3e3395-ec0c-48fc-86ef-d8277855f5d5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect pixel art to real-world design by noticing how artists use a small number of squares, colors, and details to make an image recognizable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Pixel artists do a lot with only a few squares. Can you identify one of the famous paintings in pixel art?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify artwork they recognize. The pixel art pieces depict (top to button, left to right): Girl with a Pearl Earring by Johannes Vermeer, Mona Lisa by Leonardo da Vinci, Sunflowers by Vincent van Gogh, Composition with Red, Blue and Yellow by Piet Mondrian, and The Starry Night by Vincent van Gogh."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e3e3395-ec0c-48fc-86ef-d8277855f5d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26911f61-2ea8-42ce-898a-f2dd2cb47643"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2997a56-e140-44a3-a560-5493bdf9375e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What clues helped you figure them out?*\n- *What details did the artist keep?*\n- *What details did they leave out?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that even simple pixel art involves intentional choices. Artists decide which colors, shapes, and key details are most important so the image is still recognizable, even when many details are removed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2997a56-e140-44a3-a560-5493bdf9375e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26911f61-2ea8-42ce-898a-f2dd2cb47643"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2876f03d-bc55-4bc2-8685-932eba90f66a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2876f03d-bc55-4bc2-8685-932eba90f66a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26911f61-2ea8-42ce-898a-f2dd2cb47643"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "275ad7c6-e8ae-4470-bc43-44018da817b8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "275ad7c6-e8ae-4470-bc43-44018da817b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26911f61-2ea8-42ce-898a-f2dd2cb47643"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ac0aed9-3b75-4403-9c5e-12e23ba7b370",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students sketch and plan their pixel art before coding so they have a clear goal to work toward.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Design Your Program activity guide and set of color pencils.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Design Your Program"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ac0aed9-3b75-4403-9c5e-12e23ba7b370",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eefa3fd9-5021-4925-a37c-9c8a2ae85aa3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f64f1d7-713a-4ed0-98ab-e1d43dbcb3ef",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you will create your own pixel art design, then use code to bring it to life. Before we code, we’re going to design. Your sketch is your plan. It should help you decide what colors you need, where your painter should move, and what parts of the image need to be painted",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f64f1d7-713a-4ed0-98ab-e1d43dbcb3ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eefa3fd9-5021-4925-a37c-9c8a2ae85aa3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f27de66-da06-4700-953f-c0e8c8e09566",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students sketch their program design on the grid and answer the planning questions.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to start simple. A clear design with a few colors is better than a very detailed design that is hard to finish in time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f27de66-da06-4700-953f-c0e8c8e09566",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eefa3fd9-5021-4925-a37c-9c8a2ae85aa3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1c8b44e-9cb7-45ad-b348-0c0feda9a2ba",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to page 2 of the activity guide. Have them review the rubric before they begin coding."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1c8b44e-9cb7-45ad-b348-0c0feda9a2ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eefa3fd9-5021-4925-a37c-9c8a2ae85aa3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d48697b-3265-4cb9-9c21-e3544b0fb1f6",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use **set\\_paint()**, **move()**, **turn\\_left()**, and **paint()** to recreate their planned pixel art design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 20, Level 1. Have students use their sketch as a guide while they code. Remind them to test often instead of waiting until the end.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are coding without checking their sketch, students who need help sequencing commands, and students who are stuck because their painter is facing the wrong direction.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Program Your Sketch",
        "progression_name": "Make: Program Your Sketch",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to check whether students can write a sequential program and debug when the output does not match their intended design."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d48697b-3265-4cb9-9c21-e3544b0fb1f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eefa3fd9-5021-4925-a37c-9c8a2ae85aa3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6206ed55-1bb7-45fe-a952-fa4c88b0c6d4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6206ed55-1bb7-45fe-a952-fa4c88b0c6d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96ca6dfa-9852-4a2e-9739-308f1b1b78f8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7cd2dc09-b504-4fbf-a9f9-61222470c483",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7cd2dc09-b504-4fbf-a9f9-61222470c483",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96ca6dfa-9852-4a2e-9739-308f1b1b78f8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a0b8ada-798f-4bd2-9e6b-bf4abb6a6e92",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students walk around or view shared projects. Ask them to notice both the artwork and the coding choices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice? What do you wonder?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students see that there can be many different successful solutions, even when everyone uses the same basic commands."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a0b8ada-798f-4bd2-9e6b-bf4abb6a6e92",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96ca6dfa-9852-4a2e-9739-308f1b1b78f8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "38e6e3e3-9b4f-4627-89a0-fadd2284fa09",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students notice that apps store personally relevant information, and that some of it changes over time while other pieces stay fixed, without naming \"variables\" yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What are some pieces of information a favorite app of yours might remember about you?*\n- *Which of those would stay the same every time you open it? Which might change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students land on the fixed vs changing distinction themselves (a username probably stays the same, a high score changes) rather than being told it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "38e6e3e3-9b4f-4627-89a0-fadd2284fa09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d45f40d8-3b16-4660-9a37-f2668be3f43d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "897498bb-fbe5-46e3-83f2-e1ef43e01fe4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "897498bb-fbe5-46e3-83f2-e1ef43e01fe4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d45f40d8-3b16-4660-9a37-f2668be3f43d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80f51c52-12ee-4c91-8b8f-8031e9bfe2a2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80f51c52-12ee-4c91-8b8f-8031e9bfe2a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d45f40d8-3b16-4660-9a37-f2668be3f43d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ed4e7b1-5983-42f4-8f45-44a971c43792",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:**  Direct students to take out their copy of the CSP Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ed4e7b1-5983-42f4-8f45-44a971c43792",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a2eecaf-b1dc-488f-90ae-5c24b6682c90",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students distinguish numbers from strings by how they're written, and understand a variable as a labeled container that holds one value at a time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a small stack of red stickies, yellow stickies, 3 plastic baggies, and a dry erase marker.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Values and Variables",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Supplies Substitutions:** There's no need to use sticky notes if you have other scraps of colored paper. Also consider cutting stickies in 4 to make them go further. If you don't have dry erase markers handy consider using pieces of masking tape on the baggies. Sticky notes do not have to be red and yellow, any two different colors will be fine as long as you tell students to be consistent in the types of data they are assigning to each sticky.\n\n**Running the Activity:** This activity asks students to follow along as a number of core concepts for programming are introduced. The model is typically that a term or concept is introduced and modeled and then afterwards students are encouraged to try it out on their own. Trying it out typically means they are writing information on a sticky note and sharing it with another group before discussing the results with the whole class.\n\nSlides with animations have an icon in the bottom left corner to let you know you need to click to reveal more of the slide's content.\n\nTo help you more easily prepare the activity and keep track of your instructions, here are some tips to help you throughout the presentation.\n\n- There are opportunities throughout the presentation for students to actively engage. At these moments students should be making things with their manipulatives or using them to answer questions. Use these opportunities to check progress.\n\n- There is a fair amount of new vocabulary introduced but it is introduced gradually and with intentional repetition. Make a point of actively modeling the use of new terms.\n\n- The most important goal here is building a mental model. It is ok if students have some open questions that will get resolved over the subsequent lessons."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a2eecaf-b1dc-488f-90ae-5c24b6682c90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5be91db-4528-4f18-ae3a-df59a0387a6d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students that today, we are going to be thinking about how computers work with information. Explain that one \"piece\" of information is a \"value\" and right now, there are two types of values: numbers and strings. Numbers work the way you normally think of numbers and are made of the digits 0 through 9. String are made of any characters you can see on the keyboard which means all the examples on the slide count as strings. Strings go inside double quotes, while numbers don't need quotes. Point out to students how it would be important to tell the number 123 from the string \"123\". Direct students to make one string and one number and hold them up at their table.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students unsure whether \"123\" counts as a number or a string, the quotes are what decide it, not the characters inside them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5be91db-4528-4f18-ae3a-df59a0387a6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd0e8156-c461-47a1-adc6-e1c2fb6d263c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain to students that each plastic baggies on their table is a \"variable\". Variables can hold at most one value, or sticky note. They have names that use no quotes, include no spaces, and must start with a letter."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd0e8156-c461-47a1-adc6-e1c2fb6d263c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b13feb84-ab7a-4754-97ca-565e0c2c4230",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students make one string variable and one number variable with any names they like and give each a value by writing a number on a sticky and placing it inside the number variable baggie and writing a string on a sticky and placing it inside the string variable baggie. Have students share the variables they made with another group. Ensure students use a whiteboard marker so you can reuse the baggies later.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a student picks an invalid name (starts with a number, includes a space), let their partner group catch it during share-out rather than correcting it yourself."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b13feb84-ab7a-4754-97ca-565e0c2c4230",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bfa2517-0565-48b9-a2b6-3f555bdb0c1e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *variable*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bfa2517-0565-48b9-a2b6-3f555bdb0c1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8c3cdfb-82bc-40ca-9209-5f259419e68c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain to students that when they put a sticky note value inside their variable baggie, they assigned a value to their variable. Click through the animated slide to demonstrate what assigning a value to a variable looks like in Python."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8c3cdfb-82bc-40ca-9209-5f259419e68c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "319aece9-6223-4190-aea1-01c85408e68c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *assignment operator*. Relate this to putting the sticky note inside the variable baggie."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "319aece9-6223-4190-aea1-01c85408e68c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4962ea9c-e633-4e5e-865a-7c7ff167fd66",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students trace a variable's value across repeated assignment and reassignment, building the tracing skills necessary for using variables effectively in their programs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that we're going to read and run programs using the code on the slides. Students will create, assign and reassign variable values by reading the programs on the slides and following them sequentially. Click the animated slide to show an example of a program and the variable baggie and sticky note that students would create to represent that line of code. Remind students that the equal sign is called the \"assignment operator\" and it is just a fancy word for \"put this value in the baggy\".",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Assigning and Tracing Variables"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4962ea9c-e633-4e5e-865a-7c7ff167fd66",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bde570c9-034e-45b3-9047-d457af1f7629",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a moment to create their baggies and sticky notes by following the program on the slide. Click through the animated slide to run the program. Explain that when we read line 01 we say \"pow gets 3\". Remind students that variables can only hold one sticky note or value, so if we try to assign a variable that already has a value in it, we just throw the old one away.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To help students understand that the variable can only hold one value at a time, which means the first value is removed from the variable and \"thrown away\", consider demonstrating exactly that in front of your class – remove the sticky note, crumple it up and throw it \"out\" (in the trash or even over your shoulder), and put the new value in the variable bag.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bde570c9-034e-45b3-9047-d457af1f7629",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92e6c773-9137-486b-a086-a0b0d7aa615e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to run this program and compare results with another group. When most students have made an attempt, click through the animated slide to display the result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Walk around the room making sure students are following the rules correctly. No baggies should have more than one sticky note in them.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Help students to notice that pizza can hold a number and then a string, nothing locks a baggie to one type."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92e6c773-9137-486b-a086-a0b0d7aa615e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3edb8ad-b6d3-4f46-90aa-f9f570950750",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students trace each variable's value after every line. When most students have made an attempt, click through the animated slide to display the result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** This is the longest trace so far, watch for students who lose track partway through and skip ahead instead of working line by line.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "While many students may want to skip straight to the end, encourage them to physically move the values, explaining that the ability to trace values over the course of a program is an essential skill in programming."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3edb8ad-b6d3-4f46-90aa-f9f570950750",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5f13981-3992-4a36-93e3-a8384dcc598e",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students trace each variable's value after every line, then compare their results with another group. When most students have made an attempt, click through the animated slide to display the result.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Line 5 may be tricky for some students. Remind students that if a value has quotation marks around it, it is a String, not a number."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity to check whether students can assign and reassign a variable's value and trace its value correctly across multiple sequential steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5f13981-3992-4a36-93e3-a8384dcc598e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f19f8bfb-fadc-44c9-b4f8-f9563aa0e5d0",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Why does storing \"1\" in lives make it a string, even though it looks like a number?*\n- *What would change about how the program treats lives if you removed the quotation marks from \"1\"?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students state, in their own words, that quotation marks are what determine a value's type, not the characters inside it, and connect that rule to something concrete, like the difference between lives = \"1\" holding a string and lives = 1 holding a number."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f19f8bfb-fadc-44c9-b4f8-f9563aa0e5d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8a57ecd1-243d-4bdd-aab2-7d63275bc5d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ad2618b-3af9-44a8-917c-57e450a4bc10",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ad2618b-3af9-44a8-917c-57e450a4bc10",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bcd339b8-0c43-435c-8518-6e06e6775ebb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87d473f8-d408-4aca-8dc9-06d857fc0e55",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87d473f8-d408-4aca-8dc9-06d857fc0e55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bcd339b8-0c43-435c-8518-6e06e6775ebb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78f851e6-722d-4187-bb10-acf655f3a784",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the blue play icon to start the Give One, Get One activity. Prompt students to find a partner and share one word or phrase they'd use to explain what a variable is, in their own words. After both students share, they should find a new partner and repeat the process. After the activity, invite students to revise their activity guide if they learned something new from a peer.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Using music to cue transitions helps build predictable routines. Let students know what the music means and be consistent in using it to launch this type of activity. **Click the blue play icon** on the slide to begin.\n\nGive One, Get One encourages students to circulate and share their findings. Each student \"gives\" an idea to a partner, then \"gets\" an idea in return. Prompt students to repeat with new partners, and encourage them to listen for different ways of describing the same idea, or details they missed."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78f851e6-722d-4187-bb10-acf655f3a784",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bcd339b8-0c43-435c-8518-6e06e6775ebb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d528436-202a-4b8f-843c-05846c638daa",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d528436-202a-4b8f-843c-05846c638daa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bcd339b8-0c43-435c-8518-6e06e6775ebb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4957fe82-f701-464c-bcc6-86b5d32fccc0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recall what they learned about combining strings and numbers, and predict how that rule extends to joining two strings together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What happens when you add a number and a string, like 5 + \"5\"?*\n- *Why do you think Python treats them differently — what's different about how you'd write a number versus a string?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recall that a program treats numbers and text differently, and connect that to a visual difference: strings are written with quotes, numbers aren't."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4957fe82-f701-464c-bcc6-86b5d32fccc0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "973df408-fb2c-407e-af63-77371d72aef1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8be51940-1c84-4698-8b9c-48c6baea700f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8be51940-1c84-4698-8b9c-48c6baea700f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "973df408-fb2c-407e-af63-77371d72aef1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be8536a2-dd3b-42a8-9cc5-b5f44406fc18",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be8536a2-dd3b-42a8-9cc5-b5f44406fc18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "973df408-fb2c-407e-af63-77371d72aef1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08b25916-63ff-44ad-8399-aa80f19324ea",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students predict what a program will do before seeing how it runs, then unpack why through annotations that name each data type, explain variable naming rules, and show how input() and print() work in practice.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Creating Purposeful Apps and Programs Unit Guide and the Tracing Variables Activity Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Predict and Run"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08b25916-63ff-44ad-8399-aa80f19324ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4297009a-8034-4a89-9bd5-67092dc34e66"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b376f1c9-fc10-467b-8856-dc04749372f5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 22, Level 1 to complete Level 1 to predict the output of the program before running it to check their prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for predictions about what each variable will print in order to identify who already has a sense of how strings, numbers, and booleans display differently.",
        "progression_name": "Predict and Run"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b376f1c9-fc10-467b-8856-dc04749372f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4297009a-8034-4a89-9bd5-67092dc34e66"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a02b27fd-6777-4c9a-94d8-9631671013cc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Discuss: How close was your prediction?*\n- *What did the program actually print for your favorite subject?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that the printed line matched whatever they personally typed — input() captures something the program didn't know in advance."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a02b27fd-6777-4c9a-94d8-9631671013cc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4297009a-8034-4a89-9bd5-67092dc34e66"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6beea1ee-ac3e-4bb1-8f67-a6c56f6e0505",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data type*. Remind students that a string is made up of characters and a number is made up of digits. Explain that the boolean data type holds either a True of False value."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6beea1ee-ac3e-4bb1-8f67-a6c56f6e0505",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4297009a-8034-4a89-9bd5-67092dc34e66"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d574e5fb-6ee6-48b8-9876-670407140777",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the annotations to show how **=** stores a value in a variable, how each data type (string, number, boolean) is written differently, and the rules for naming a variable.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Read **=** as \"stores\" or \"gets,\" **not** \"equals\" — it's putting a value into the variable, not checking if two things are equal. This is also a good moment to point out that **is_a_student** uses an underscore instead of a space, since variable names can't contain spaces."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d574e5fb-6ee6-48b8-9876-670407140777",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4297009a-8034-4a89-9bd5-67092dc34e66"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "355550ed-8947-4175-b3f5-5c83aa05385c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the annotations to show how input() waits for and stores what the user types, then point to the console output to show how commas print multiple values in one line, separated by a space.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that print() can take more than two arguments. Here it's four, alternating text and variables. Students don't need to memorize this pattern yet, just recognize that each comma adds another item to the line."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "355550ed-8947-4175-b3f5-5c83aa05385c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4297009a-8034-4a89-9bd5-67092dc34e66"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1525f54-f2a0-49b7-94d9-001e12c7c5fc",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply what the annotation slides just taught, first by tracing and testing existing code, then by building something personal with it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 3.\n- **Level 2:** Students investigate the code and comment on what each line does.\n- **Level 3:** Students personalize their own badge using what they learned.\n- **Tracing Variables Activity Guide:** Students trace a program in order to show what value each variable holds after each line executes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students' comments correctly describe what input() does and correctly identify each variable's data type. On Level 3, confirm each student's badge includes their own string, int, and boolean variable, each printed with commas.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Investigate and Modify",
        "progression_name": "Investigate and Modify",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 3 and the Tracing Variables Activity Guide can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1525f54-f2a0-49b7-94d9-001e12c7c5fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4297009a-8034-4a89-9bd5-67092dc34e66"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd58ef3d-2a5e-41f0-86d4-cd42594c307d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What did you add to make your badge your own?*\n- *What's one thing the AI Tutor helped you check or fix?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to name a specific variable they added and describe in their own words what it does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd58ef3d-2a5e-41f0-86d4-cd42594c307d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4297009a-8034-4a89-9bd5-67092dc34e66"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15cf385a-731e-4a8d-958d-db4727690589",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
