        "lesson_activity.key": "f988c5ef-267b-454d-b8cd-da06807c6f23"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70157fba-6ba0-42fe-97ed-db69857e5799",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today. Facilitate a think-pair-share to discuss students' responses."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70157fba-6ba0-42fe-97ed-db69857e5799",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f988c5ef-267b-454d-b8cd-da06807c6f23"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "edadbb33-d127-4ab5-969f-5b6959b3edf2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**  Students connect today's sketch-then-build structure to real design practice before they see either level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Before a programmer writes code, they often sketch a plan first. Why might that help?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students surface ideas like catching problems early, deciding what a project needs before typing, or having something to check the finished code against."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "edadbb33-d127-4ab5-969f-5b6959b3edf2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "09e0c9ad-3c8b-4910-8894-a9ccfa6b0d79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dcc6c286-af74-4aa7-b948-957dba409e9a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dcc6c286-af74-4aa7-b948-957dba409e9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "09e0c9ad-3c8b-4910-8894-a9ccfa6b0d79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e8539f6-8fed-4a60-9f02-6ab2b0e1772e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e8539f6-8fed-4a60-9f02-6ab2b0e1772e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "09e0c9ad-3c8b-4910-8894-a9ccfa6b0d79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c884535-d8b7-43ce-9e20-4d23dc35a703",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Rubric - Variables Make"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c884535-d8b7-43ce-9e20-4d23dc35a703",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eee36d3c-daa2-4a55-ade5-2b86c6b2a9c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09b63571-cfa1-463d-8789-b30911a09546",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students plan a personally meaningful scene by labeling five variables (three strings, one int, one bool), then bring that plan to life in code, handing off to the AI Tutor to generate the visual.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 23, Level 1 to complete levels 1-2.\n- **Level 1:** Students place five color-coded label boxes onto a sketch (three string, one int, one bool), then draw arrows connecting each label to its part of the scene.\n- **Level 2:** Students turn their sketch into variables (**input()** for the **strings**, direct assignment for the **int** and **bool**), reassign one, and print them all. The AI Tutor then builds the visual from their code and sketch, which students predict, run, and evaluate before documenting what they kept, changed, or rejected.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** By roughly the 10-minute mark, most students should be moving from Level 1 into Level 2 — flag anyone still finalizing their sketch well past that point. In Level 2, check that string variables use input() and that the int/bool do not, and confirm the reassignment line updates a variable already created above it, not a brand-new variable. For the AI Tutor step, watch for students running before predicting, redirect them to write a prediction first.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Plan, Then Build",
        "progression_name": "Sketch and Make",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may try to use **input()** for their int and bool variables. If asked why those two don't use **input()**, explain that **input()** always returns text: a value like 5 would be stored as the string \"5\" rather than the number 5. Working around this is covered in a later lesson."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to check whether students can correctly assign variables by type, reassign a variable's value, and evaluate and document AI-generated code against their own design."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09b63571-cfa1-463d-8789-b30911a09546",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eee36d3c-daa2-4a55-ade5-2b86c6b2a9c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3edc781a-2c8c-4169-aa86-cedcb1c88e0e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3edc781a-2c8c-4169-aa86-cedcb1c88e0e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c341b2d6-ce33-4502-a44d-374d4b84ec93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dcaea2e6-7c76-41fd-a609-74d517eeb97c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dcaea2e6-7c76-41fd-a609-74d517eeb97c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c341b2d6-ce33-4502-a44d-374d4b84ec93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54e7d3be-f273-4f10-b925-599789b63ce1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did you keep, change, or reject from the AI's code, and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reference a specific line or value (a color, a shape, a variable) rather than a general \"it worked\" or \"it didn't.\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54e7d3be-f273-4f10-b925-599789b63ce1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c341b2d6-ce33-4502-a44d-374d4b84ec93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64d32e6b-74dd-42fc-b791-25b5151dbf50",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recall Day 2's warm-up prediction (combining a number and a string) and extend that same predicting habit to a new case — combining two strings — surfacing that + behaves differently depending on data type, without naming why yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts and reveal each line's output only after students share their predictions.\n- *What do you think line 1 will print?*\n- *What do you think line 2 will print?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice **5 + 3** gives 8 (adds), but **\"5\" + \"3\"** gives \"53\" (joins) — same symbol, different result depending on the data type. Do not introduce the word \"concatenation\" here; let it stay an open notice heading into Predict & Run.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Resist revealing both console lines at once — even a few seconds of holding the answer back is what makes this a prediction rather than a readout. If time is short, a quick thumbs-up/thumbs-down vote on each guess before revealing keeps it fast without skipping the predict step."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64d32e6b-74dd-42fc-b791-25b5151dbf50",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91c061e7-4521-4def-a3db-266f9d2981d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1938731f-233d-48cf-b56b-814eeb8bb47f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1938731f-233d-48cf-b56b-814eeb8bb47f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91c061e7-4521-4def-a3db-266f9d2981d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1207d6a-8f16-4725-ab9b-18e34bfaa6ae",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1207d6a-8f16-4725-ab9b-18e34bfaa6ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91c061e7-4521-4def-a3db-266f9d2981d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70819530-62bc-487a-944a-9fb2c9fdfa18",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70819530-62bc-487a-944a-9fb2c9fdfa18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14a3518f-f625-48e4-bc7a-4e2e76e92be6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students predict what the Spy Code Name program outputs before running it, using concatenation and substring syntax they haven't yet been taught by name.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 24, Level 1 to predict the output of a program that builds a \"code name\" by combining a substring of a pet name with a substring of a birth month, then run the program to check their prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for how students reason about the unfamiliar bracket notation (**pet_name[0:3]**) — are they guessing randomly, or trying to infer a pattern from the numbers? Note any students who confidently name what they expect versus those who seem stuck, since this can help you group pairs for the Notice/Wonder discussion that follows.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Predict & Run",
        "progression_name": "Predict & Run"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14a3518f-f625-48e4-bc7a-4e2e76e92be6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7387a8bf-f918-443c-a04f-ac69c97f401a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What do you notice about the code in this program?*\n- *What do you wonder about the code in this program?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students describe in their own words what the code seemed to do — for example, that it grabbed a few letters from each variable and joined them together — without needing the terms \"concatenation\" or \"substring\" yet. Surface genuine wonderings (e.g., \"why 3 letters from one and 2 from the other?\") and let them stay open; these get addressed in Investigate, not answered here."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7387a8bf-f918-443c-a04f-ac69c97f401a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "323a5834-c9a5-40a8-a3df-a559cc7e0228",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have each student take out their a copy of the unit guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "323a5834-c9a5-40a8-a3df-a559cc7e0228",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed60afc3-5acf-4a01-93cc-6ed2b56e4104",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *string*. Remind students they've been using strings since the first Intro to Programming lesson with the Painter and the Neighborhood (e.g., paint(\"white\")) and again throughout the Variables lessons."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed60afc3-5acf-4a01-93cc-6ed2b56e4104",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54df605d-51e8-4886-a0da-55a1bf5533b4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *concatenation*. Point out both **+** symbols and how each one joins two pieces together.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Notice the two + symbols do different jobs — the first builds a new string and saves it in code_name; the second builds a string just for what gets printed. We'll dig into that difference more on the next slide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54df605d-51e8-4886-a0da-55a1bf5533b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26db68ff-078a-46fb-b126-f645a3b831c9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide. Have students discuss with a partner what sticky they think lands in the greeting baggie, then reveal the answer (\"hi Ally!\"). If students don't predict correctly, use it to surface the two most common errors: forgetting the space inside \"hi \" (giving \"hiAlly!\"), and treating first as the literal text \"first\" instead of substituting its stored value.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a pair predicts correctly right away, ask them what would happen if \"hi \" were written as \"hi\" (no space) — this previews the space-dropping error as a quick follow-up rather than only introducing it as a hypothetical if no one makes that mistake."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26db68ff-078a-46fb-b126-f645a3b831c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14658d9b-cdd8-499b-b4ed-fe5c20182682",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to reveal the first code example (lines 1–2). Give students a moment to discuss with a partner what they think the output will be before clicking to reveal it and the comma-spacing annotation. Point out that line 1 has two comma spots that don't look the same — \"I was born in \" already ends with a space, so the comma's automatic space stacks on top, creating a double space before July; \"and I am\" has no trailing space, so you only see the comma's single space before 16.  Repeat for the second example (lines 3–4): pause for a prediction before revealing the output and the concatenation annotation. Line 3's **+** doesn't add anything automatically at all — that's why \"hi \" needs its own trailing space, or the output would read \"hiAlly!\".",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask \"why not just use commas for everything?\" — the biggest reason is that comma-print only displays values once; it doesn't create anything you can save or reuse. Concatenation with + builds an actual string value you can store in a variable, like code_name in the Spy Code Name program, and use again later. As a smaller practical note: comma-print also adds spacing automatically (which can create surprises like a double space), while + never adds spacing on its own, so you're always in full control."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14658d9b-cdd8-499b-b4ed-fe5c20182682",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fbb28aa-51a5-4290-bbc8-c96b8abe693f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test the existing code name formula against edge-case inputs — a short name, an empty string, and a single space — to discover how slicing behaves when there isn't much (or anything) to grab, without changing the formula itself.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 24, Level 2 to complete the investigate steps.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for whether students correctly distinguish Step 2's empty string from Step 3's single space — this is the most likely point of confusion. Also watch for students who expect Step 1 (short name) to throw an error; if they're surprised it doesn't, that's the exact discovery this activity is built around.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Investigate Strings",
        "progression_name": "Investigate",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2 can be used as a formative check for the \"distinguish empty strings from strings with spaces\" objective — listen for whether students can articulate the Step 2 vs. Step 3 difference in their own words during Circulate, even though this level itself isn't a written check."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fbb28aa-51a5-4290-bbc8-c96b8abe693f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d6b4e03-eae6-4b09-afe0-e25b24b7bb2b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *substring*. Remind students this is the same thing they were just testing in Investigate — **pet_name[0:3]** grabbing part of \"Whiskers\" — now with a name attached. Give students a moment to discuss with a partner what they think **birth_month[0:2]** will grab, then reveal \"Oc\"."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d6b4e03-eae6-4b09-afe0-e25b24b7bb2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d14e465c-9048-424a-af95-c29b3ddb82d2",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how **pet_name[0:3]** matches the numbers to the highlighted letters. Point out that 0 lands right before the W and 3 lands right after the i — not on a letter itself — which is why the slice stops at \"Whi\" instead of grabbing the s.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The numbers label the gaps between characters, not the characters themselves — this is the most common point of confusion with slicing. If a student expects index 3 to point at the 4th letter, use the diagram to show it sits just after the i (end of \"Whi\"), which is why **[0:3]** stops there instead of including the s."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d14e465c-9048-424a-af95-c29b3ddb82d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74d29f6c-4857-43a2-89b6-24e486562e31",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students 2–3 minutes to write their predictions for all five slices in their Unit Guide, then have them compare answers with a partner before any are revealed. Cold-call students for their prediction on each item, one at a time, and after a few responses click to reveal that item's answer before moving to the next. For each reveal, ask a student to explain why the slice returned that result before moving on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for whether students are just guessing versus reasoning through start/end positions using the \"How Substrings Work\" diagram from the previous slide. Item 4 (pet_name[2:2]) and item 5 (pet_name[1:100]) are the most likely to trip students up — watch for partners who disagree on these two specifically, since that's a good signal for who to cold-call on them.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Items 4 and 5 are the trickiest — pet_name[2:2] returns an empty string (not an error, since start and end are the same position), and pet_name[1:100] doesn't break even though 100 is far past the end of the string. If students are unsure, connect it back to Investigate: strings don't throw errors just because there isn't much (or anything) left to grab."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74d29f6c-4857-43a2-89b6-24e486562e31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6fa5a18f-142b-475c-a969-f939357ae4c0",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 3 to answer the check for understanding questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For Question 1, watch whether students trace the substring and concatenation separately or try to guess the output as a whole — if they're stuck, point them back to the \"How Substrings Work\" diagram rather than re-explaining it from scratch. For Question 2, listen for whether students' explanations name the actual distinction (an empty string has zero characters; a space is still one character) rather than just asserting the values are \"different\" without saying why.",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Both questions on Level 3 serve as the formative check for this lesson's two objectives — Question 1 checks whether students can trace code that combines concatenation and substrings to determine the displayed output (1.6.C.4/.5), and Question 2 checks whether students can distinguish an empty string from a string containing a space and explain the difference in their own words (1.6.C.3)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6fa5a18f-142b-475c-a969-f939357ae4c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e48a129b-6ec0-4555-ae74-ee55f29dba17"
      }
    },
